=begin
  highlights.lic  --  WHO PROFESSION  ->  name highlights in Warlock or Wrayth
  (formerly whohl.lic)

  Runs WHO PROFESSION <profession>, collects the names, and gives each person a
  name highlight in that profession's text and background colors, in whichever
  front end you're playing in. Names you added yourself are never changed.
  Re-running a scan ADDS new names (people who were offline last time are kept).

  Warlock 3.1+: one entry per person in Settings -> Names, for the character you're
    logged in as (--global: for all characters, under Global). Warlock reloads the
    file on its own, so the colors show up right away.
  Saga: one highlight class per profession (in its colors) with a highlight per
    person. Saga syncs its settings online and ignores outside edits, so this writes
    a Saga settings file to import (in your Lich folder: saga-settings-highlights-<name>.json;
    Saga: Menu -> Import Settings, Clear and overwrite). It holds only highlights: your current
    ones plus the scanned names, so nothing else in Saga changes. Import it before
    changing highlights in Saga (or rebuild it with ;highlights reapply).
  Wrayth: one entry per person in this character's Wrayth names, in Wrayth's
    settings file (%APPDATA%\StormFront\<game>\<player id>.xml; Wrayth's settings
    location must be set to local). Wrayth only reads that file when it starts, so
    new names show up the next time you log in. Colors use the closest color in
    your Wrayth palette. The file is backed up before every change (last 10 kept,
    in Lich's data folder under highlights\backups).

  Usage:
    ;highlights wizard                 scan WHO PROFESSION WIZARD and save the names
    ;highlights wizard #ff5599ff       same, with your own text color
    ;highlights wizard --bg=#ff202060  ...and/or a background color (--bg=none for none)
    ;highlights wizard --fresh         replace the list instead of adding to it
    ;highlights wizard --dry           show the names found, write nothing
    ;highlights gamehost               GameHosts on duty (from a plain WHO's Staff on duty)
    ;highlights gamemaster             GameMasters on duty (any prefix, e.g. Senior)
    ;highlights staff                  both of those, from one WHO
    ;highlights all                    scan every profession, then staff
    ;highlights add wizard Name Name   add names by hand
    ;highlights drop wizard Name       remove names by hand
    ;highlights remove wizard          delete all of that profession's names
    ;highlights list                   show what's saved
    ;highlights where                  show which front end and file are being used
    ;highlights setup                  pick each profession's text and background colors
    ;highlights reapply                put all saved names back (and recolor them)
    ;highlights cleanup                Wrayth: remove blank entries from the names list
    ;highlights spells                 Warlock: color this character's spell bars (Active Spells,
                                       Buffs, Debuffs, Cooldowns) by spell circle, using its colors
                                       from setup's Spell circles tab. Bars you colored yourself
                                       that match their circle are taken over; others are left
                                       alone unless you add --replace.
    ;highlights spells off             take this script's colors off the spell bars
    ;highlights spells list            show this character's circle colors
    ;highlights watch                  Wrayth: keep running; if Wrayth saves over the
                                       scanned names, put them back right away
    --global                           Warlock only: save for all characters
    --fe=warlock / wrayth / saga       if the front end isn't detected

  Color codes are Warlock's #AARRGGBB (AA is opacity; ff = solid), so you can paste
  them straight from Warlock. Wrayth's 6-digit #RRGGBB works too and is taken as solid.
  Only players with FLAG ShowProfession ON appear in WHO PROFESSION.

  If Warlock's settings folder isn't found, set WARLOCK_CONFIG to it, or use
  --dir="C:\path\to\folder".

  author: Peggyanne
  version: 2.3.0

  changes:
    2.3.0  Spell circles (Warlock): bar, background and text colors per spell circle plus
           society abilities, per character, in a new setup tab; ;highlights spells.
    2.2.3  Saga: instructions say to import with Clear and overwrite, and to import before changing
           highlights in Saga (or rebuild the file with reapply first).
    2.2.2  Saga: the import file is named saga-settings-highlights-<name>.json; Saga only
           reads files whose names start with saga-settings as its own settings.
    2.2.1  Saga: the import file now has the empty "globals" section and \n line endings
           Saga's own highlights-only export has (Saga rejected it without them).
    2.2.0  Saga: builds a highlights-only Saga settings file to import (one class per
           profession, your own highlights kept). Detected automatically.
    2.1.0  GameHost and GameMaster: their own colors in setup and their own lists, from
           the Staff on duty part of a plain WHO (any capitalization, any prefix such
           as Senior). ;highlights gamehost, gamemaster, staff; all includes them.
    2.0.5  Someone already saved under another profession (e.g. they changed
           profession) is left alone and you're told, with the commands to move them.
           Warlock used to end up with two Names entries for them.
    2.0.4  Wrayth: writes <names> compact (no line breaks between entries). The line breaks
           2.0.0-2.0.3 put there showed up in Wrayth as a blank entry between every
           name; any blank entries are removed the next time the file is written.
           New ;highlights cleanup does just that.
    2.0.3  Wrayth: reads the palette and names however Wrayth writes them (single or double
           quotes, <h/> or <h></h>), and refuses to write if any names entry wasn't
           understood, so none of your names can be lost.
    2.0.2  Wrayth: reads and writes the settings file in whatever encoding Wrayth saved it
           in (UTF-16 or UTF-8), so its <names> section is found.
    2.0.1  Wrayth: when a character has settings files for more than one game (e.g. Prime
           GS4 and the test server GST), use the one for the game you're logged in to.
    2.0.0  Renamed highlights.lic. Works with Wrayth too: detects which front end
           you're using and saves there. Wrayth names go in its settings file, using
           your palette's closest colors; new reapply and watch commands. A new
           Warlock character folder is named from Warlock's own character list
           (Lich's game name is spelled differently, so 1.4.0 could guess wrong).
    1.4.0  Saves for the character you're logged in as by default. --global saves
           for all characters instead (replaces --me, which is now the default).
    1.3.1  Fixed for Windows: folder searches (--me, moving 1.2.x highlights into Names,
           and Save + recolor Warlock) found nothing because of backslashes in the
           path. --me also works for a character Warlock has no folder for yet.
    1.3.0  One entry per person, in Warlock's Names list (Settings -> Names) instead
           of one regex highlight per profession. Regex highlights from 1.2.x are
           moved over automatically and removed. Your own Names entries are left alone.
    1.2.2  Colors now really save: they go in whohl_colors.json in Lich's data folder
           (Lich's Settings didn't save from the setup window). Setup has
           "Save colors" (just remember them) and "Save + recolor Warlock" (also
           update saved highlights), and warns before closing with unsaved changes.
    1.2.1  Codes now match Warlock: #AARRGGBB, pasted straight from Warlock (8 digits
           used to be cut off to 7, so #ff2999a1 came out pink). #RRGGBB from Wrayth
           still works.
    1.2.0  Background colors: each profession has a text color and a background
           color (or none), in ;whohl setup and with --bg= on the command line.
    1.1.0  ;whohl setup window: color wheel or #RRGGBB code per profession, with
           an example line in each color. Saving recolors highlights already in
           Warlock. A color given on the command line is remembered too.
           Savant removed until it's a real profession.
    1.0.0  first release
=end

require 'digest/md5'
require 'time'
require 'json'
require 'fileutils'

module WhoHL
  # Savant is left out until it's a playable profession; add it here (and a color below) then.
  PROFESSIONS = %w[bard cleric empath monk paladin ranger rogue sorcerer warrior wizard].freeze
  # Staff on duty, from a plain WHO ("GameHost Myerz", "Senior GameMaster ...").
  STAFF = %w[gamehost gamemaster].freeze
  # Everything that gets its own colors and its own list.
  CATEGORIES = (PROFESSIONS + STAFF).freeze
  LABELS = { 'gamehost' => 'GameHost', 'gamemaster' => 'GameMaster' }.freeze

  def self.label(category)
    LABELS[category] || category.capitalize
  end

  DEFAULT_COLORS = {
    'bard'     => '#ff66cc', 'cleric'  => '#66ccff', 'empath'   => '#66ff99',
    'monk'     => '#ffcc66', 'paladin' => '#ffffff', 'ranger'   => '#99cc33',
    'rogue'    => '#cc99ff', 'sorcerer' => '#ff5555',
    'warrior'  => '#ff9933', 'wizard'  => '#5599ff',
    'gamehost' => '#ffd700', 'gamemaster' => '#ff4500',
  }.freeze

  # Words that can appear on WHO header/footer lines and are never player names.
  NOT_NAMES = %w[
    Active Players Player Brave Adventurers Adventurer Questing Staff Senior GameHost
    GameMaster Use Who WHO HELP Total There None No Displaying Profession Professions
  ].freeze

  class Error < StandardError; end

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- Warlock files
  module Warlock
    module_function

    # Windows paths come with backslashes, which Ruby's Dir.glob reads as escape characters, so every
    # folder search silently found nothing. Forward slashes work everywhere on Windows.
    def config_dir(override = nil)
      find_config_dir(override).tr('\\', '/')
    end

    def find_config_dir(override = nil)
      if override && !override.empty?
        return override if File.directory?(override)
        raise Error, "Warlock folder not found: #{override}"
      end
      env = ENV['WARLOCK_CONFIG']
      return env if env && File.directory?(env)

      home = Dir.home rescue ENV['HOME'] || ENV['USERPROFILE']
      candidates = []
      candidates << File.join(ENV['LOCALAPPDATA'], 'WarlockFE', 'warlock') if ENV['LOCALAPPDATA']
      candidates << File.join(ENV['APPDATA'], 'WarlockFE', 'warlock') if ENV['APPDATA']
      candidates << File.join(home, 'Library', 'Application Support', 'warlock')
      candidates << File.join(home, 'Library', 'Preferences', 'warlock')
      candidates << File.join(ENV['XDG_CONFIG_HOME'] || File.join(home, '.config'), 'warlock')

      found = candidates.find do |d|
        File.directory?(File.join(d, 'characters')) || File.exist?(File.join(d, 'client.toml'))
      end
      return found if found

      raise Error, "Couldn't find Warlock 3's settings folder. Looked in:\n  " +
                   candidates.join("\n  ") +
                   "\nOpen Warlock 3.1 or newer once, or point at the folder with --dir=\"...\""
    end

    # Same rule Warlock uses for folder and lock-file names.
    def sanitize(s)
      s.each_char.map { |c| c =~ /[A-Za-z0-9\-_.]/ ? c : '_' }.join
    end

    # Returns [character_id, folder]. nil character => the shared "global" folder.
    # Returns [character_id, folder]. nil character => the shared "global" folder. The folder may
    # not exist yet: Warlock only makes it once something is saved for that character, so whohl
    # makes it when it saves. game_hint is the game code Lich reports (e.g. GS3), used only to
    # choose between games when the same name is in more than one.
    def target(dir, character = nil, game_hint: nil)
      chars = File.join(dir, 'characters')
      return ['global', File.join(chars, 'global')] if character.nil?

      name = sanitize(character.downcase)
      games = Dir.glob(File.join(chars, '*', name)).select { |d| File.directory?(d) }
                 .map { |d| File.basename(File.dirname(d)) }
      # No folder yet: use the game from Warlock's own character list. (Lich's XMLData.game is
      # spelled differently, e.g. GSIV, so it can't be used to name the folder.)
      games = games_in_connections(dir, character) if games.empty?
      if games.size > 1 && game_hint
        pick = games.select { |g| g.casecmp?(game_hint.to_s) }
        games = pick unless pick.empty?
      end
      if games.empty?
        raise Error, "Warlock doesn't list #{character} among its characters (checked connections.toml), so I can't " \
                     "tell which game folder to use. Log #{character} in through Warlock once, or use --global to save for all characters."
      end
      raise Error, "#{character} is in more than one game in Warlock (#{games.join(', ')}); log in as them so I can tell which." if games.size > 1
      game = sanitize(games.first.downcase)
      ["#{game}:#{name}", File.join(chars, game, name)]
    end

    # Game codes Warlock has for this character in connections.toml ([[characters]] and [[connections]]).
    def games_in_connections(dir, character)
      path = File.join(dir, 'connections.toml')
      return [] unless File.exist?(path)
      text = File.read(path, mode: 'rb').force_encoding('UTF-8')
      text.split(/^\s*\[\[\s*(?:characters|connections)\s*\]\]\s*$/).filter_map do |block|
        # A [[characters]] id is Warlock's own "<game>:<name>", the folder name; prefer it.
        id = block[/^\s*id\s*=\s*["']([^"':]+):([^"']*)["']/, 0] && [$1, $2]
        next id[0].downcase if id && id[1].casecmp?(character)
        who = block[/^\s*character\s*=\s*["']([^"']*)["']/, 1] || block[/^\s*name\s*=\s*["']([^"']*)["']/, 1]
        game = block[/^\s*gameCode\s*=\s*["']([^"']*)["']/, 1]
        game.downcase if who && game && !game.empty? && who.casecmp?(character)
      end.uniq
    end

    # Hold the same lock file Warlock holds while it writes this character's files.
    def with_lock(dir, character_id)
      chars = File.join(dir, 'characters')
      Dir.mkdir(chars) unless File.directory?(chars)
      File.open(File.join(chars, ".#{sanitize(character_id)}.lock"), File::RDWR | File::CREAT) do |f|
        f.flock(File::LOCK_EX)
        begin
          yield
        ensure
          f.flock(File::LOCK_UN)
        end
      end
    end

    def write_atomic(path, text)
      tmp = path + '.whohl.tmp'
      File.open(tmp, 'wb') { |f| f.write(text) }
      File.rename(tmp, path)
    end
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------ highlights.toml
  # Edits highlights.toml as text so no TOML gem is needed. The file is a list of
  # [[highlights]] blocks; we only touch blocks whose id is one of ours and leave
  # every other highlight (and its comments) exactly as it was.
  class HighlightsFile
    HEADER = /^\s*\[\[\s*highlights\s*\]\]\s*$/

    attr_reader :path

    def initialize(path)
      @path = path
      @text = File.exist?(path) ? File.read(path, mode: 'rb').force_encoding('UTF-8') : ''
      if @text =~ /^\s*highlights\s*=/
        raise Error, "#{path} stores highlights in a form this script doesn't edit (highlights = [...]). " \
                     'Nothing was changed.'
      end
      split
    end

    def self.id_for(profession)
      h = Digest::MD5.hexdigest("whohl:#{profession}")
      h[12] = '3'                                    # UUID version 3 (name-based)
      h[16] = %w[8 9 a b][h[16].to_i(16) & 3]        # RFC 4122 variant
      "#{h[0, 8]}-#{h[8, 4]}-#{h[12, 4]}-#{h[16, 4]}-#{h[20, 12]}"
    end

    def names(profession)
      block = find(profession)
      return [] unless block
      m = block.match(/^\s*pattern\s*=\s*"\\\\b\(\?:(.*)\)\\\\b"\s*$/)
      m ? m[1].split('|') : []
    end

    # {'text' => '#rrggbb', 'bg' => '#rrggbb' or 'none'} of a saved profession, or nil.
    def style(profession)
      block = find(profession)
      return nil unless block
      read = lambda do |key|
        v = block[/#{key}\s*=\s*"([^"]*)"/, 1].to_s
        Colors.canon(v)
      end
      { 'text' => read.('textColor'), 'bg' => read.('backgroundColor') || 'none' }
    end

    # Change the colors of a saved profession highlight. Returns true if anything changed.
    def recolor(profession, style)
      old = self.style(profession)
      return false if old.nil? || old == Colors.normalize(style)
      set(profession, names(profession), style)
      true
    end

    def saved_professions
      CATEGORIES.select { |p| find(p) }
    end

    # style: {'text' => '#rrggbb', 'bg' => '#rrggbb' or 'none'}
    def set(profession, names, style)
      id = self.class.id_for(profession)
      names = names.uniq.sort
      @blocks.reject! { |b| owns?(b, id) }
      return if names.empty?
      style = Colors.normalize(style)
      Colors.check!(profession => style)
      text = style['text']
      bg = style['bg'] == 'none' ? 'default' : style['bg']
      @blocks << <<~TOML
        [[highlights]]
        # whohl: #{profession} (#{names.size} names, updated #{Time.now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M')})
        id = "#{id}"
        pattern = "\\\\b(?:#{names.join('|')})\\\\b"
        isRegex = true
        matchPartialWord = false
        ignoreCase = false
        styles = [ { group = 0, textColor = "#{text}", backgroundColor = "#{bg}", bold = false } ]

      TOML
    end

    def to_s
      body = @blocks.map { |b| b.rstrip + "\n\n" }.join
      pre = @preamble.strip.empty? ? '' : @preamble.rstrip + "\n\n"
      pre + body
    end

    private

    def split
      @preamble = +''
      @blocks = []
      @text.each_line do |line|
        if line =~ HEADER
          @blocks << line.dup
        elsif @blocks.empty?
          @preamble << line
        else
          @blocks.last << line
        end
      end
    end

    def find(profession)
      id = self.class.id_for(profession)
      @blocks.find { |b| owns?(b, id) }
    end

    def owns?(block, id)
      block =~ /^\s*id\s*=\s*["']#{Regexp.escape(id)}["']\s*$/
    end
  end

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- names.toml
  # Warlock's Names list (Settings -> Names): one entry per person, matched as a whole word,
  # with its own text and background color. Each entry this script writes has an id made from
  # the profession and the name, so it can tell its entries apart (and which profession each
  # one is) without touching names you added yourself.
  class NamesFile
    HEADER = /^\s*\[\[\s*names\s*\]\]\s*$/

    attr_reader :path

    def initialize(path)
      @path = path
      @text = File.exist?(path) ? File.read(path, mode: 'rb').force_encoding('UTF-8') : ''
      if @text =~ /^\s*names\s*=/
        raise Error, "#{path} stores names in a form this script doesn't edit (names = [...]). Nothing was changed."
      end
      split
    end

    def self.id_for(profession, name)
      h = Digest::MD5.hexdigest("whohl:#{profession}:#{name}")
      h[12] = '3'
      h[16] = %w[8 9 a b][h[16].to_i(16) & 3]
      "#{h[0, 8]}-#{h[8, 4]}-#{h[12, 4]}-#{h[16, 4]}-#{h[20, 12]}"
    end

    def names(profession)
      owned(profession).map { |b| field(b, 'text') }.sort
    end

    def style(profession)
      b = owned(profession).first
      return nil unless b
      { 'text' => Colors.canon(field(b, 'textColor')) || canon_default(profession),
        'bg' => Colors.canon(field(b, 'backgroundColor').to_s.sub('default', 'none')) || 'none' }
    end

    def saved_professions
      CATEGORIES.select { |p| owned(p).any? }
    end

    # {name => profession they're saved under} for names the last set found saved under another
    # profession and left alone.
    def conflicts
      @conflicts || {}
    end

    # Names already in the list that this script didn't add (yours). They're left alone.
    def users_names
      @blocks.reject { |b| profession_of(b) }.map { |b| field(b, 'text') }.compact
    end

    # Make this profession's entries exactly `names` in `style`. Returns the names skipped
    # because you already have your own entry for them.
    def set(profession, names, style)
      style = Colors.normalize(style)
      names = names.uniq.sort
      Colors.check!(profession => style) unless names.empty?
      mine = users_names
      skipped = names & mine
      # Someone already saved under another profession (they changed profession, or a list is
      # wrong) is left where they are; the caller tells you. Nothing moves on its own.
      @conflicts = {}
      @blocks.each do |b|
        q = profession_of(b)
        n = field(b, 'text')
        @conflicts[n] = q if q && q != profession && names.include?(n)
      end
      keep = names - mine - @conflicts.keys
      # Keep each entry's place in the list; replace in place, add new ones at the end.
      existing = owned(profession).to_h { |b| [field(b, 'text'), b] }
      @blocks = @blocks.filter_map do |b|
        q = profession_of(b)
        next b unless q == profession
        n = field(b, 'text')
        keep.include?(n) ? entry(profession, n, style) : nil
      end
      (keep - existing.keys).each { |n| @blocks << entry(profession, n, style) }
      skipped
    end

    def recolor(profession, style)
      old = self.style(profession)
      return false if old.nil? || old == Colors.normalize(style)
      set(profession, names(profession), style)
      true
    end

    def to_s
      body = @blocks.map { |b| b.rstrip + "\n\n" }.join
      pre = @preamble.strip.empty? ? '' : @preamble.rstrip + "\n\n"
      pre + body
    end

    private

    def canon_default(profession)
      Colors.canon(DEFAULT_COLORS[profession])
    end

    def entry(profession, name, style)
      bg = style['bg'] == 'none' ? 'default' : style['bg']
      <<~TOML
        [[names]]
        # whohl: #{profession}
        id = "#{self.class.id_for(profession, name)}"
        text = "#{name}"
        textColor = "#{style['text']}"
        backgroundColor = "#{bg}"
        bold = false
      TOML
    end

    def field(block, key)
      block[/^\s*#{key}\s*=\s*["']([^"']*)["']/, 1]
    end

    # Which profession an entry belongs to, if this script wrote it.
    def profession_of(block)
      id = field(block, 'id')
      name = field(block, 'text')
      return nil unless id && name
      CATEGORIES.find { |p| self.class.id_for(p, name) == id }
    end

    def owned(profession)
      @blocks.select { |b| profession_of(b) == profession }
    end

    def split
      @preamble = +''
      @blocks = []
      @text.each_line do |line|
        if line =~ HEADER
          @blocks << line.dup
        elsif @blocks.empty?
          @preamble << line
        else
          @blocks.last << line
        end
      end
    end
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------- colors
  # The color picked for each profession. Kept in Lich's Settings for this script, so it's the
  # same for every character on this computer.
  module Colors
    module_function

    DEFAULT_BG = 'none'

    # Codes are kept the way Warlock writes them: #AARRGGBB (AA = opacity, ff = solid).
    # A 6-digit #RRGGBB (Wrayth's form) is taken as solid.
    def valid?(c)
      c.is_a?(String) && c.strip =~ /\A#?(?:[0-9a-fA-F]{6}|[0-9a-fA-F]{8})\z/
    end

    # '#aarrggbb' for a valid code, 'none' for none, nil otherwise.
    def canon(c)
      c = c.to_s.strip.downcase
      return 'none' if c == 'none'
      return nil unless valid?(c)
      c = c.delete('#')
      "##{c.size == 6 ? 'ff' + c : c}"
    end

    # Just the color part, '#rrggbb', for previews.
    def rgb(c)
      "##{canon(c)[-6..]}"
    end

    def valid_bg?(c)
      c == 'none' || valid?(c)
    end

    # Accepts the old saved form (just a text color) as well as {'text' =>, 'bg' =>}.
    def normalize(style, profession = nil)
      style = { 'text' => style } if style.is_a?(String)
      style = (style || {}).to_h.transform_keys(&:to_s)
      text = style['text'].to_s.strip.downcase
      bg = style['bg'].to_s.strip.downcase
      text = DEFAULT_COLORS[profession] if profession && text.empty?
      bg = DEFAULT_BG if bg.empty?
      { 'text' => canon(text) || text, 'bg' => canon(bg) || bg }
    end

    def check!(styles)
      bad = styles.flat_map do |p, st|
        out = []
        out << "#{p} text" unless valid?(st['text'])
        out << "#{p} background" unless valid_bg?(st['bg'])
        out
      end
      raise Error, "Bad color for #{bad.join(', ')}; use #AARRGGBB like Warlock (#ff5599ff) or #RRGGBB like Wrayth (background can also be none)" unless bad.empty?
    end

    # Colors live in their own file in Lich's data folder, so they're the same for every character
    # and saving works from the setup window (Lich's Settings only save from the script's own thread).
    def path
      return ENV['WHOHL_COLORS'] if ENV['WHOHL_COLORS'] && !ENV['WHOHL_COLORS'].empty?
      base = defined?(::DATA_DIR) ? ::DATA_DIR : (defined?(::LICH_DIR) ? File.join(::LICH_DIR, 'data') : Dir.home)
      File.join(base, 'highlights_colors.json')
    end

    def old_path
      File.join(File.dirname(path), 'whohl_colors.json')
    end

    def saved
      file = File.exist?(path) ? path : (File.exist?(old_path) ? old_path : nil)   # whohl.lic saved to old_path
      if file
        data = begin
          JSON.parse(File.read(file))
        rescue JSON::ParserError => e
          raise Error, "Your saved colors file can't be read (#{e.message[0, 80]}). Fix or delete #{file}"
        end
        return data.is_a?(Hash) ? data : {}
      end
      # Colors saved by 1.1.0-1.2.1 went to Lich's Settings; pick them up if they're there.
      old = (::Settings['colors'] rescue nil) if defined?(::Settings)
      old.respond_to?(:to_h) ? old.to_h : {}
    end

    def all
      saved = self.saved
      CATEGORIES.to_h do |p|
        st = normalize(saved.to_h[p], p)
        st['text'] = canon(DEFAULT_COLORS[p]) unless valid?(st['text'])
        st['bg'] = DEFAULT_BG unless valid_bg?(st['bg'])
        [p, st]
      end
    end

    def get(profession)
      all[profession]
    end

    def save(styles)
      styles = styles.to_h { |p, st| [p, normalize(st, p)] }
      check!(styles)
      merged = all.merge(styles)
      dir = File.dirname(path)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir) unless File.directory?(dir)
      tmp = path + '.tmp'
      File.write(tmp, JSON.pretty_generate(merged) + "\n")
      File.rename(tmp, path)
      merged
    rescue SystemCallError => e
      raise Error, "Couldn't save colors to #{path}: #{e.message}"
    end

    # Change just the text or background of one profession; nil leaves that part alone.
    def set(profession, text: nil, bg: nil)
      st = get(profession).dup
      st['text'] = canon(text) if text
      st['bg'] = canon(bg) if bg
      save(profession => st)
      st
    end
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------- saving
  # Opens one character's (or global's) names.toml under Warlock's lock, lets the block change it,
  # then writes it back. Regex highlights from whohl 1.2.x in that folder's highlights.toml are
  # moved into names.toml first and removed from highlights.toml. Returns [names path, block
  # result, number of professions moved].
  def self.with_names(dir, char_id, folder)
    names_path = File.join(folder, 'names.toml')
    hl_path = File.join(folder, 'highlights.toml')
    result = nil
    moved = 0
    Warlock.with_lock(dir, char_id) do
      unless File.directory?(folder)
        FileUtils.mkdir_p(folder)
        # Warlock starts watching a new folder when it sees it appear; give it a moment so it
        # doesn't miss the file we're about to write there.
        sleep 1.5
      end
      names = NamesFile.new(names_path)
      before = names.to_s
      hl = File.exist?(hl_path) ? HighlightsFile.new(hl_path) : nil
      if hl
        hl.saved_professions.each do |p|
          names.set(p, names.names(p) | hl.names(p), names.style(p) || hl.style(p))
          hl.set(p, [], nil)
          moved += 1
        end
      end
      result = yield names
      Warlock.write_atomic(names_path, names.to_s) if names.to_s != before
      Warlock.write_atomic(hl_path, hl.to_s) if moved > 0
    end
    [names_path, result, moved]
  end

  def self.update(dir:, character:, profession:, game_hint: nil, &block)
    char_id, folder = Warlock.target(dir, character, game_hint: game_hint)
    path, result, = with_names(dir, char_id, folder, &block)
    [path, result]
  end

  # Every Warlock settings folder that has names or highlights in it: [[char_id, folder], ...]
  def self.all_folders(dir)
    chars = File.join(dir, 'characters')
    Dir.glob(File.join(chars, '**', '{names,highlights}.toml')).map { |f| File.dirname(f) }.uniq.map do |folder|
      parent = File.dirname(folder)
      [parent == chars ? File.basename(folder) : "#{File.basename(parent)}:#{File.basename(folder)}", folder]
    end
  end

  # Recolor every name this script saved, everywhere. Returns the number of files changed.
  def self.recolor_all(dir, colors)
    all_folders(dir).count do |char_id, folder|
      _, hits, moved = with_names(dir, char_id, folder) do |names|
        colors.count { |p, c| names.recolor(p, c) }
      end
      hits > 0 || moved > 0
    end
  end

  # Move any whohl 1.2.x regex highlights into Names, everywhere. Returns professions moved.
  def self.migrate_all(dir)
    all_folders(dir).sum { |char_id, folder| with_names(dir, char_id, folder) { nil }[2] }
  end

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- Wrayth
  # Wrayth keeps each character's settings in %APPDATA%\StormFront\<game>\<player id>.xml (when
  # its settings location is set to local). Name highlights are <h text= color= bgcolor=/> entries
  # in <names>; colors are palette slots ("@90" = <palette><i id="90" color="#FFFFFF"/>).
  # Wrayth reads the file when it starts and writes it at login and whenever a setting changes,
  # so anything written while it's open shows up at the next login (unless Wrayth saves over it
  # first; see reapply and watch).
  module Wrayth
    module_function

    def stormfront_dir
      base = ENV['APPDATA'] || File.join(Dir.home, 'AppData', 'Roaming')
      File.join(base, 'StormFront').tr('\\', '/')
    end

    # Wrayth's folder names for the game Lich says you're in. Lich renames the server's instance
    # (GS4 -> GSIV, GSX/GS4X -> GSPlat) and keeps the rest (GST, GSF, DR, ...).
    def game_folders(lich_game)
      case lich_game.to_s
      when 'GSIV' then %w[GS4 GS3]
      when 'GSPlat' then %w[GSX GS4X]
      when '' then []
      else [lich_game.to_s]
      end
    end

    def settings_path(player_id, lich_game = nil)
      id = player_id.to_s
      raise Error, "Lich doesn't have this character's player ID yet; wait until you're fully logged in." unless id =~ /\A\d+\z/
      hits = Dir.glob(File.join(stormfront_dir, '*', "#{id}.xml"))
      if hits.empty?
        raise Error, "No Wrayth settings file for this character (looked for #{File.join(stormfront_dir, '*', "#{id}.xml")}). " \
                     "In Wrayth, set the settings location to local, then log in once."
      end
      if hits.size > 1
        want = game_folders(lich_game).map(&:downcase)
        pick = hits.select { |h| want.include?(File.basename(File.dirname(h)).downcase) }
        return pick.first if pick.size == 1
        raise Error, "More than one Wrayth settings file for this character (#{hits.join(', ')}) and Lich's game " \
                     "(#{lich_game.to_s.empty? ? 'unknown' : lich_game}) doesn't pick one."
      end
      hits.first
    end

    def data_dir
      base = defined?(::DATA_DIR) ? ::DATA_DIR : Dir.home
      File.join(base.to_s.tr('\\', '/'), 'highlights')
    end

    # The names this script added, per profession, so your own names are never touched.
    # One list per settings file: <game folder>-<player id>, e.g. wrayth-GS4-1067835.json
    def owned_path(settings_file)
      game = File.basename(File.dirname(settings_file))
      File.join(data_dir, "wrayth-#{game}-#{File.basename(settings_file, '.xml')}.json")
    end

    def load_owned(settings_file)
      path = owned_path(settings_file)
      return {} unless File.exist?(path)
      data = JSON.parse(File.read(path))
      data.is_a?(Hash) ? data.transform_values { |v| Array(v).map(&:to_s) } : {}
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      raise Error, "Can't read #{path} (#{e.message[0, 60]}). Fix or delete it."
    end

    def save_owned(settings_file, owned)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(data_dir)
      path = owned_path(settings_file)
      File.write(path + '.tmp', JSON.pretty_generate(owned.reject { |_, v| v.empty? }) + "\n")
      File.rename(path + '.tmp', path)
    end

    def backup(path)
      dir = File.join(data_dir, 'backups')
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir)
      base = "#{File.basename(File.dirname(path))}-#{File.basename(path, '.xml')}"
      first = File.join(dir, "#{base}-original.xml")      # the file before this script ever changed it
      FileUtils.cp(path, first) unless File.exist?(first)
      FileUtils.cp(path, File.join(dir, "#{base}-#{Time.now.strftime('%Y%m%d-%H%M%S-%L')}.xml"))
      old = Dir.glob(File.join(dir, "#{base}-2*.xml")).sort
      old[0...-10].each { |f| File.delete(f) rescue nil } if old.size > 10   # keep the last 10
    end

    def write(path, text)
      tmp = path + '.highlights.tmp'
      File.binwrite(tmp, text)
      tries = 0
      begin
        File.rename(tmp, path)
      rescue SystemCallError
        tries += 1
        if tries < 6
          sleep 0.3
          retry
        end
        File.binwrite(path, text)        # Wrayth had the file open; write over it in place instead
        File.delete(tmp) rescue nil
      end
    end
  end

  # The <names> section of one Wrayth settings file, edited as text so the rest of the file stays
  # exactly as Wrayth wrote it. Same interface as NamesFile.
  class WraythFile
    # <h .../> or <h ...></h>, with "double" or 'single' quotes.
    ENTRY = /<h\b[^>]*?(?:\/>|>.*?<\/h\s*>)/m
    ITEM = /<i\b[^>]*?(?:\/>|>.*?<\/i\s*>)/m

    attr_reader :path, :owned, :slots, :blanks_removed, :compacted, :raw

    def initialize(path, owned)
      @path = path
      @owned = owned.transform_values(&:dup)
      @raw = File.binread(path)
      @bom, @encoding = self.class.detect_encoding(@raw)
      @text = @raw.byteslice(@bom.bytesize..).force_encoding(@encoding).encode('UTF-8')
      m = @text.match(/<names\b[^>]*\/>|<names\b[^>]*>(.*?)<\/names>/m)
      unless m
        raise Error, "#{path} has no <names> section that this script can read (read it as #{@encoding}" \
                     "#{@bom.empty? ? '' : ' with a BOM'}; it starts with #{@raw.byteslice(0, 12).unpack1('H*')}). Nothing was changed."
      end
      @names_open = m[0][/\A<names\b[^>]*?(?=\s*\/?>)/]
      @names_span = [m.begin(0), m.end(0)]
      inner = m[1] || ''
      @entries = inner.scan(ENTRY)
      # Refuse to rebuild the section unless every entry in it was understood: anything else in
      # there (another kind of tag, a comment) would be lost, and so would your names.
      leftover = inner.gsub(ENTRY, '').strip
      count = inner.scan(/<h\b/).size
      if !leftover.empty? || count != @entries.size
        raise Error, "#{path}'s <names> section has entries this script doesn't understand " \
                     "(#{count} found, #{@entries.size} read; first unread part: #{leftover[0, 80].inspect}). Nothing was changed."
      end
      # Wrayth writes <names> with nothing between entries, and reads any whitespace in there as
      # blank entries, so the section is always written compact. Entries with no name text (the
      # blanks 2.0.0-2.0.3 caused that way) are dropped.
      before_count = @entries.size
      @entries.reject! { |e| attr(e, 'text').to_s.strip.empty? }
      @blanks_removed = before_count - @entries.size
      @compacted = !inner.gsub(ENTRY, '').empty?
      pal = @text[/<palette\b[^>]*>(.*?)<\/palette\s*>/m, 1].to_s
      @palette = pal.scan(ITEM).filter_map do |i|
        id = attr(i, 'id')
        c = attr(i, 'color').to_s[/\A#?(?:[0-9a-fA-F]{2})?([0-9a-fA-F]{6})\z/, 1]
        [id, "##{c.upcase}"] if id && c
      end
      if @palette.empty?
        start = @text.index('<palette')
        raise Error, "#{path} has no palette colors this script can read (starts: " \
                     "#{start ? @text[start, 120].inspect : 'no <palette> tag'}). Nothing was changed."
      end
      @slots = {}
    end

    def names(profession)
      (@owned[profession] || []).sort
    end

    def style(_profession)
      nil
    end

    def saved_professions
      CATEGORIES.select { |p| @owned[p]&.any? }
    end

    # The palette slot closest to a color, as [id, '#RRGGBB'].
    def slot_for(hex)
      r, g, b = hex.delete('#')[-6..].scan(/../).map { |c| c.to_i(16) }
      @palette.min_by do |_, c|
        r2, g2, b2 = c[1..].scan(/../).map { |x| x.to_i(16) }
        rm = (r + r2) / 2.0
        (2 + rm / 256) * (r - r2)**2 + 4 * (g - g2)**2 + (2 + (255 - rm) / 256) * (b - b2)**2
      end
    end

    def set(profession, names, style)
      style = Colors.normalize(style)
      names = names.uniq.sort
      Colors.check!(profession => style) unless names.empty?
      mine = @owned.values.flatten
      # Someone already saved under another profession is left where they are; the caller tells you.
      @conflicts = {}
      @owned.each { |q, list| list.each { |n| @conflicts[n] = q if q != profession && names.include?(n) } }
      present = @entries.to_h { |e| [attr(e, 'text'), e] }
      skipped = names.select { |n| present.key?(n) && !mine.include?(n) }
      keep = names - skipped - @conflicts.keys
      old = @owned[profession] || []
      # Drop this profession's old entries, then add the current ones.
      @entries.reject! { |e| old.include?(attr(e, 'text')) || keep.include?(attr(e, 'text')) && mine.include?(attr(e, 'text')) }
      unless keep.empty?
        fg = slot_for(style['text'])
        bg = style['bg'] == 'none' ? nil : slot_for(style['bg'])
        @slots[profession] = { 'text' => fg, 'bg' => bg }
        keep.each { |n| @entries << %(<h color="@#{fg[0]}" text="#{n}" bgcolor="#{bg ? "@#{bg[0]}" : ''}" />) }
      end
      @owned.each { |q, list| list.reject! { |n| keep.include?(n) } unless q == profession }
      @owned[profession] = keep
      skipped
    end

    # {name => profession they're saved under} for names the last set found saved under another
    # profession and left alone.
    def conflicts
      @conflicts || {}
    end

    def recolor(profession, style)
      return false if names(profession).empty?
      set(profession, names(profession), style)
      true
    end

    # Names this script added that aren't in the file (Wrayth saved over them).
    def missing
      present = @entries.map { |e| attr(e, 'text') }
      @owned.values.flatten - present
    end

    # The file as bytes, in the encoding (and with the byte-order mark) Wrayth wrote it in.
    def to_bytes
      @bom.b + to_s.encode(@encoding).b
    end

    # [byte-order mark, encoding]. Wrayth may write UTF-16 (two bytes per character).
    def self.detect_encoding(raw)
      head = raw.byteslice(0, 4).b
      return ["\xFF\xFE".b, 'UTF-16LE'] if head.start_with?("\xFF\xFE".b)
      return ["\xFE\xFF".b, 'UTF-16BE'] if head.start_with?("\xFE\xFF".b)
      return ["\xEF\xBB\xBF".b, 'UTF-8'] if head.start_with?("\xEF\xBB\xBF".b)
      sample = raw.byteslice(0, 200).b
      if sample.count("\x00") > sample.size / 4
        return ['', sample.getbyte(0).zero? ? 'UTF-16BE' : 'UTF-16LE']
      end
      ['', 'UTF-8']
    end

    def to_s
      body = @entries.empty? ? "#{@names_open} />" : "#{@names_open}>#{@entries.join}</names>"
      @text[0...@names_span[0]] + body + @text[@names_span[1]..]
    end

    private

    def attr(entry, key)
      m = entry.match(/\b#{key}\s*=\s*(["'])(.*?)\1/m)
      m && m[2]
    end
  end

  # Opens the logged-in character's Wrayth file, lets the block change it, then backs up and
  # writes the file and the list of names this script owns. Returns [path, block result].
  def self.wrayth_update(player_id, game = nil)
    path = Wrayth.settings_path(player_id, game)
    file = WraythFile.new(path, Wrayth.load_owned(path))
    result = yield file
    after = file.to_bytes
    if after != file.raw.b                     # compare with what's on disk, so a cleanup alone is written too
      Wrayth.backup(path)
      Wrayth.write(path, after)
    end
    Wrayth.save_owned(path, file.owned)
    [path, result, file]
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------- Saga
  # Saga keeps settings in %APPDATA%\saga\prefs.json, per character ("subeta|prime"), and syncs
  # them online: it loads the online copy when it starts, so editing prefs.json is undone. Its
  # own Import Settings is the way in. This writes a Saga settings file holding only the
  # "highlights" category: your current highlights and classes (from prefs.json) plus one class
  # per profession with a highlight per name. Importing it is a normal change Saga keeps and syncs.
  module Saga
    module_function

    ID_PREFIX = 'hlic-'          # ids this script writes: hlic-g-wizard, hlic-h-wizard-alba

    def prefs_path
      base = ENV['APPDATA'] || File.join(Dir.home, 'AppData', 'Roaming')
      File.join(base, 'saga', 'prefs.json').tr('\\', '/')
    end

    def read_prefs
      path = prefs_path
      raise Error, "Saga's settings file isn't where it was expected (#{path})." unless File.exist?(path)
      JSON.parse(File.read(path, encoding: 'UTF-8'))
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      raise Error, "Can't read #{path} (#{e.message[0, 60]}). Nothing was changed."
    end

    # Saga's names for the game, after the character: "subeta|prime".
    def game_words(lich_game)
      case lich_game.to_s
      when 'GSIV' then %w[prime]
      when 'GSPlat' then %w[platinum plat]
      when 'GSF' then %w[shattered]
      when 'GST' then %w[test]
      else [lich_game.to_s.downcase]
      end
    end

    def character_key(prefs, name, lich_game)
      keys = (prefs['characterSettings'] || {}).keys.select { |k| k.split('|').first.to_s.casecmp?(name) }
      raise Error, "Saga has no settings for #{name} yet; log #{name} in with Saga once." if keys.empty?
      return keys.first if keys.size == 1
      want = game_words(lich_game)
      pick = keys.select { |k| want.include?(k.split('|', 2)[1].to_s.downcase) }
      return pick.first if pick.size == 1
      raise Error, "Saga has #{name} in more than one game (#{keys.join(', ')}) and Lich's game (#{lich_game}) doesn't pick one."
    end

    def ours?(item)
      item['id'].to_s.start_with?(ID_PREFIX)
    end

    def group_id(category)
      "#{ID_PREFIX}g-#{category}"
    end

    def highlight_id(category, name)
      "#{ID_PREFIX}h-#{category}-#{name.downcase}"
    end

    # Warlock-style #AARRGGBB -> Saga's #RRGGBB (solid) or #RRGGBBAA.
    def color(code)
      c = Colors.canon(code)
      return nil if c.nil? || c == 'none'
      alpha = c[1, 2]
      "##{c[3, 6]}#{alpha == 'ff' ? '' : alpha}"
    end

    def data_dir
      Wrayth.data_dir
    end

    def owned_path(key)
      File.join(data_dir, "saga-#{key.tr('|', '-')}.json")
    end

    def load_owned(key)
      path = owned_path(key)
      return {} unless File.exist?(path)
      data = JSON.parse(File.read(path))
      data.is_a?(Hash) ? data.transform_values { |v| Array(v).map(&:to_s) } : {}
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      raise Error, "Can't read #{path} (#{e.message[0, 60]}). Fix or delete it."
    end

    def save_owned(key, owned)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(data_dir)
      path = owned_path(key)
      File.write(path + '.tmp', JSON.pretty_generate(owned.reject { |_, v| v.empty? }) + "\n")
      File.rename(path + '.tmp', path)
    end

    def import_dir
      dir = defined?(::LICH_DIR) ? ::LICH_DIR.to_s : File.dirname(data_dir)
      dir.tr('\\', '/')
    end

    def import_path(key)
      # Saga picks the importer from the file name: only names starting with saga-settings are read
      # as Saga settings (anything else is tried as a Wrayth .xml export).
      File.join(import_dir, "saga-settings-highlights-#{key.split('|').first}.json")
    end
  end

  # One character's Saga highlights: what Saga has now (from prefs.json) plus what this script
  # wants there. Same interface as NamesFile.
  class SagaFile
    attr_reader :key, :owned

    def initialize(prefs, key, owned)
      @key = key
      @owned = owned.transform_values(&:dup)
      settings = prefs.dig('characterSettings', key) || {}
      @highlights = Array(settings['customHighlights'])
      @groups = Array(settings['highlightGroups'])
      @styles = {}
    end

    def names(category)
      (@owned[category] || []).sort
    end

    def style(_category)
      nil
    end

    def saved_professions
      CATEGORIES.select { |c| @owned[c]&.any? }
    end

    def conflicts
      @conflicts || {}
    end

    # Your own highlights (not this script's), by pattern.
    def users_patterns
      @highlights.reject { |h| Saga.ours?(h) }.map { |h| h['pattern'].to_s }
    end

    def set(category, names, style)
      style = Colors.normalize(style)
      names = names.uniq.sort
      Colors.check!(category => style) unless names.empty?
      @conflicts = {}
      @owned.each { |q, list| list.each { |n| @conflicts[n] = q if q != category && names.include?(n) } }
      mine = users_patterns
      skipped = names.select { |n| mine.include?(n) }
      @owned[category] = names - skipped - @conflicts.keys
      @styles[category] = style
      skipped
    end

    def recolor(category, style)
      return false if names(category).empty?
      @styles[category] = Colors.normalize(style)
      true
    end

    # Names this script wants in Saga that Saga doesn't have yet (not imported, or imported and
    # then removed).
    def missing
      have = @highlights.select { |h| Saga.ours?(h) }.map { |h| h['pattern'] }
      @owned.values.flatten - have
    end

    # The Saga settings file to import: your highlights and classes as they are, with this
    # script's classes and names replaced by what it wants now.
    def export(colors)
      groups = @groups.reject { |g| Saga.ours?(g) }.map(&:dup)
      highlights = @highlights.reject { |h| Saga.ours?(h) }.map(&:dup)
      saved_professions.each do |c|
        st = @styles[c] || colors[c]
        g = { 'id' => Saga.group_id(c), 'name' => WhoHL.label(c), 'enabled' => true, 'collapsed' => true,
              'color' => Saga.color(st['text']) }
        bg = Saga.color(st['bg'])
        g['background'] = bg if bg
        groups << g
        names(c).each do |n|
          highlights << { 'id' => Saga.highlight_id(c, n), 'pattern' => n, 'groupId' => g['id'], 'scope' => 'match',
                          'wholeWord' => true, 'caseSensitive' => true, 'highlightType' => 'general', 'enabled' => true }
        end
      end
      { 'kind' => 'saga-settings', 'version' => 1, 'exportedAt' => Time.now.utc.iso8601(3), 'character' => @key,
        'categories' => ['highlights'],
        'characterSettings' => { 'customHighlights' => highlights, 'highlightGroups' => groups },
        'globals' => {} }    # a highlights-only Saga export has an empty globals; Saga won't take the file without it
    end
  end

  # Builds the Saga import file for the logged-in character. Returns [import path, block result, file].
  def self.saga_update(name, game)
    prefs = Saga.read_prefs
    key = Saga.character_key(prefs, name, game)
    file = SagaFile.new(prefs, key, Saga.load_owned(key))
    result = yield file
    path = Saga.import_path(key)
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(path))
    # Binary mode, so Windows doesn't turn \n into \r\n: byte-for-byte the layout Saga's own export uses.
    File.binwrite(path + '.tmp', JSON.pretty_generate(file.export(Colors.all)) + "\n")
    File.rename(path + '.tmp', path)
    Saga.save_owned(key, file.owned)
    [path, result, file]
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------ spell circles
  # Warlock colors each bar in Active Spells, Buffs, Debuffs and Cooldowns by the bar's id from the
  # game, kept per character in progressbars.toml ([progressBars.<id>] with barColor,
  # backgroundColor, textColor). For spells the id is the spell number, so a circle is a range of
  # numbers. Society abilities use the game's own ids: Voln symbols ~3000s, Council of Light signs
  # ~5000s, Guardians of Sunfist sigils ~5300s (not Lich's 9700-9900 numbers).
  module SpellCircles
    module_function

    CIRCLES = [
      ['minor_spiritual', 'Minor Spiritual',   [100..199]],
      ['major_spiritual', 'Major Spiritual',   [200..299]],
      ['cleric',          'Cleric',            [300..399]],
      ['minor_elemental', 'Minor Elemental',   [400..499]],
      ['major_elemental', 'Major Elemental',   [500..599]],
      ['ranger',          'Ranger',            [600..699]],
      ['sorcerer',        'Sorcerer',          [700..799]],
      ['wizard',          'Wizard',            [900..999]],
      ['bard',            'Bard',              [1000..1099]],
      ['empath',          'Empath',            [1100..1199]],
      ['minor_mental',    'Minor Mental',      [1200..1299]],
      ['paladin',         'Paladin',           [1600..1699]],
      ['arcane',          'Arcane',            [1700..1799]],
      ['society',         'Society abilities', [3000..3040, 5000..5030, 5300..5330]],
    ].freeze
    KEYS = CIRCLES.map(&:first).freeze

    DEFAULT_BARS = {
      'minor_spiritual' => '#fff8ff9c', 'major_spiritual' => '#ffffffff', 'cleric' => '#ffe0e0e0',
      'minor_elemental' => '#ff4eb4ff', 'major_elemental' => '#ff2611ff', 'ranger' => '#ff2e9330',
      'sorcerer' => '#ff8b1a1a', 'wizard' => '#ffff1c38', 'bard' => '#ffff66cc', 'empath' => '#ffcd43ff',
      'minor_mental' => '#ffff7c20', 'paladin' => '#ff5f0098', 'arcane' => '#ffff3000', 'society' => '#ffd4af37',
    }.freeze

    def label(key)
      CIRCLES.find { |k, _, _| k == key }&.at(1) || key
    end

    def default_style(key)
      { 'bar' => DEFAULT_BARS[key], 'bg' => 'none', 'text' => 'none' }
    end

    def defaults
      KEYS.to_h { |k| [k, default_style(k)] }
    end

    def circle_of(id)
      n = id.to_s.to_i
      return nil unless id.to_s =~ /\A\d+\z/
      CIRCLES.find { |_, _, ranges| ranges.any? { |r| r.cover?(n) } }&.first
    end

    # The bar ids a circle covers: the spells Lich knows in that circle when a spell list is given
    # (fewer entries), otherwise every number in the range. Society abilities always use the ranges.
    def ids_for(key, spell_numbers = nil)
      ranges = CIRCLES.find { |k, _, _| k == key }[2]
      if key != 'society' && spell_numbers && !spell_numbers.empty?
        spell_numbers.select { |n| ranges.any? { |r| r.cover?(n) } }.sort.map(&:to_s)
      else
        ranges.flat_map(&:to_a).map(&:to_s)
      end
    end

    # A circle's colors: bar, background and text, each '#aarrggbb' or 'none' (Warlock's default).
    def normalize(st)
      st = (st || {}).to_h.transform_keys(&:to_s)
      %w[bar bg text].to_h { |p| [p, Colors.canon(st[p].to_s.empty? ? 'none' : st[p]) || st[p].to_s] }
    end

    def check!(styles)
      bad = styles.flat_map { |k, st| %w[bar bg text].reject { |p| Colors.valid_bg?(st[p]) }.map { |p| "#{label(k)} #{p}" } }
      raise Error, "Bad color for #{bad.join(', ')}; use #AARRGGBB, #RRGGBB or none" unless bad.empty?
    end

    # Per-character circle colors, kept in Lich's data folder: { "gs3:peggyanne" => { circle => style } }
    def path
      File.join(Wrayth.data_dir, 'spell_colors.json')
    end

    def all_saved
      return {} unless File.exist?(path)
      data = JSON.parse(File.read(path))
      data.is_a?(Hash) ? data : {}
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      raise Error, "Can't read #{path} (#{e.message[0, 60]}). Fix or delete it."
    end

    def saved(char_id)
      s = all_saved[char_id]
      s.is_a?(Hash) ? KEYS.to_h { |k| [k, s[k] ? normalize(s[k]) : default_style(k)] } : nil
    end

    def save(char_id, styles)
      styles = styles.to_h { |k, st| [k, normalize(st)] }
      check!(styles)
      data = all_saved.merge(char_id => styles)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.dirname(path))
      File.write(path + '.tmp', JSON.pretty_generate(data) + "\n")
      File.rename(path + '.tmp', path)
      styles
    end

    # Starting colors for a character with nothing saved: for each circle, the colors most of its
    # bars already have in Warlock (so colors set by hand carry over), else the defaults.
    def seed(file)
      KEYS.to_h do |k|
        seen = file.ids.select { |id| circle_of(id) == k }.map { |id| file.entry(id) }
                   .reject { |e| e.values.all? { |v| v == 'none' } }
        best = seen.group_by(&:itself).max_by { |_, v| v.size }&.first
        [k, best || default_style(k)]
      end
    end

    # The bars this script has colored, per character:
    #   { "written" => { id => style it wrote }, "original" => { id => your colors before it took over } }
    def owned_path(char_id)
      File.join(Wrayth.data_dir, "warlock-bars-#{Warlock.sanitize(char_id)}.json")
    end

    def load_owned(char_id)
      p = owned_path(char_id)
      return {} unless File.exist?(p)
      data = JSON.parse(File.read(p))
      data.is_a?(Hash) ? { 'written' => data['written'] || {}, 'original' => data['original'] || {} } : {}
    rescue JSON::ParserError => e
      raise Error, "Can't read #{p} (#{e.message[0, 60]}). Fix or delete it."
    end

    def save_owned(char_id, owned)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(Wrayth.data_dir)
      p = owned_path(char_id)
      File.write(p + '.tmp', JSON.pretty_generate(owned) + "\n")
      File.rename(p + '.tmp', p)
    end
  end

  # Warlock's progressbars.toml, edited as text: a [progressBars] line, then one
  # [progressBars.<id>] block per bar. Blocks are replaced in place; new ones go at the end.
  class ProgressBarsFile
    HEADER = /^\s*\[\s*progressBars\s*\.\s*(?:"([^"]+)"|'([^']+)'|([A-Za-z0-9_-]+))\s*\]\s*$/
    FIELDS = { 'bar' => 'barColor', 'bg' => 'backgroundColor', 'text' => 'textColor' }.freeze

    def initialize(path)
      @path = path
      text = File.exist?(path) ? File.read(path, mode: 'rb').force_encoding('UTF-8') : ''
      if text =~ /^\s*progressBars\s*=/
        raise Error, "#{path} stores bar colors in a form this script doesn't edit. Nothing was changed."
      end
      @preamble = +''
      @blocks = {}                      # id => block text, in file order
      current = nil
      text.each_line do |line|
        if (m = line.match(HEADER))
          current = m[1] || m[2] || m[3]
          @blocks[current] = line.dup
        elsif current
          @blocks[current] << line
        else
          @preamble << line
        end
      end
      @preamble = "[progressBars]\n" unless @preamble =~ /^\s*\[\s*progressBars\s*\]/
    end

    def ids
      @blocks.keys
    end

    # { 'bar' =>, 'bg' =>, 'text' => } with 'none' for Warlock's "default"; nil if no block.
    def entry(id)
      b = @blocks[id.to_s] or return nil
      FIELDS.to_h do |k, key|
        v = b[/^\s*#{key}\s*=\s*["']([^"']*)["']/, 1].to_s
        [k, v.empty? || v == 'default' ? 'none' : (Colors.canon(v) || v)]
      end
    end

    def set(id, style)
      lines = FIELDS.map { |k, key| %(#{key} = "#{style[k] == 'none' ? 'default' : style[k]}") }
      old = @blocks[id.to_s]
      # Keep anything else in an existing block (e.g. a font), replacing only the three colors.
      if old
        body = old.lines.drop(1).reject { |l| FIELDS.values.any? { |key| l =~ /^\s*#{key}\s*=/ } }
        body.pop while body.last&.strip&.empty?
        @blocks[id.to_s] = "[progressBars.#{id}]\n" + lines.join("\n") + "\n" + body.join
      else
        @blocks[id.to_s] = "[progressBars.#{id}]\n" + lines.join("\n") + "\n"
      end
    end

    def delete(id)
      @blocks.delete(id.to_s)
    end

    def to_s
      ([@preamble.rstrip] + @blocks.values.map(&:rstrip)).join("\n\n") + "\n"
    end
  end

  # Colors a character's spell bars by circle. Bars this script colored before (and you haven't
  # changed since) are updated; uncolored bars are colored; bars you colored that already match
  # their circle are taken over; bars you colored differently are left alone unless replace.
  # styles nil = take this script's colors off instead. Returns a report hash.
  # previous: the circle colors before this change (saved, or seeded from your Warlock colors). A bar
  # you colored that matches its circle's previous colors follows the circle to the new ones.
  def self.apply_spell_colors(dir, char_id, folder, styles, spell_numbers, replace: false, previous: nil)
    path = File.join(folder, 'progressbars.toml')
    report = { written: 0, adopted: 0, kept: [], removed: 0, path: path }
    Warlock.with_lock(dir, char_id) do
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(folder)
      file = ProgressBarsFile.new(path)
      before = file.to_s
      data = SpellCircles.load_owned(char_id)
      owned = data['written'] || {}
      original = data['original'] || {}
      if styles.nil?
        # Off: bars this script made are removed; bars you had colored get your colors back.
        # Bars you've changed since are left as you have them.
        owned.each do |id, st|
          next unless file.entry(id) == st
          original[id] ? file.set(id, original[id]) : file.delete(id)
          report[:removed] += 1
        end
        owned = {}
        original = {}
      else
        SpellCircles::KEYS.each do |k|
          want = SpellCircles.normalize(styles[k])
          was = previous && previous[k] ? SpellCircles.normalize(previous[k]) : nil
          SpellCircles.ids_for(k, spell_numbers).each do |id|
            cur = file.entry(id)
            mine = owned[id] && owned[id] == cur
            if cur == want
              unless owned[id] == want
                report[:adopted] += 1
                original[id] ||= cur unless owned[id]
              end
              owned[id] = want
            elsif cur.nil? || cur.values.all? { |v| v == 'none' } || mine || replace || (was && cur == was)
              original[id] ||= cur if cur && !owned[id] && !cur.values.all? { |v| v == 'none' }
              file.set(id, want)
              owned[id] = want
              report[:written] += 1
            else
              report[:kept] << id
            end
          end
        end
      end
      Warlock.write_atomic(path, file.to_s) if file.to_s != before
      SpellCircles.save_owned(char_id, { 'written' => owned, 'original' => original })
    end
    report
  end

  # Warlock characters that have a settings folder: [[char_id, label, folder], ...]
  def self.warlock_characters(dir)
    Dir.glob(File.join(dir, 'characters', '*', '*')).select { |d| File.directory?(d) }.filter_map do |d|
      game = File.basename(File.dirname(d))
      next if game == 'global' || File.basename(d) == 'global'
      name = File.basename(d)
      ["#{game}:#{name}", "#{name.capitalize} (#{game})", d]
    end.sort_by { |c| c[1] }
  end

  # ------------------------------------------------------------- which front end
  # Lich can't tell Warlock from Wrayth by itself (Warlock connects as a Wrayth-style front end
  # and even sends Wrayth's handshake), so look at the front end's program name.
  def self.frontend(override = nil)
    return override.downcase.to_sym if override
    fe = ($frontend rescue nil).to_s.downcase
    return :saga if fe == 'saga'
    if fe =~ /wizard|avalon|genie|profanity|frostbite/
      raise Error, "#{fe} isn't supported yet; this works with Warlock, Wrayth and Saga for now."
    end
    mod = (defined?(::Frontend) && ::Frontend.respond_to?(:pid)) ? ::Frontend : (defined?(::Lich::Common::Frontend) ? ::Lich::Common::Frontend : nil)
    pid = (mod.pid rescue nil).to_i
    name = process_name(pid) if pid > 0
    return :warlock if name.to_s =~ /warlock/i
    return :wrayth if name.to_s =~ /wrayth|stormfront/i
    running = running_frontends
    return running.first if running.size == 1
    raise Error, "Couldn't tell whether you're using Warlock or Wrayth. Add --fe=warlock or --fe=wrayth."
  end

  def self.windows?
    RUBY_PLATFORM =~ /mswin|mingw|cygwin/ ? true : false
  end

  # Windows process names through WMI, the way Lich looks them up (no console window flashes).
  def self.wmi_names(where = nil)
    require 'win32ole'
    wmi = WIN32OLE.connect('winmgmts://')
    rows = wmi.ExecQuery("SELECT Name FROM Win32_Process#{where ? " WHERE #{where}" : ''}")
    names = []
    rows.each { |r| names << r.Name.to_s }
    names
  rescue StandardError, LoadError
    nil
  end

  def self.process_name(pid)
    if windows?
      wmi = wmi_names("ProcessId=#{pid.to_i}")
      return wmi.first if wmi
      `tasklist /FI "PID eq #{pid.to_i}" /FO CSV /NH 2>NUL`.to_s[/\A"([^"]+)"/, 1]
    else
      `ps -p #{pid.to_i} -o comm= 2>/dev/null`.to_s.strip
    end
  rescue StandardError
    nil
  end

  def self.running_frontends
    list = if windows?
             (wmi_names || []).join("\n").then { |w| w.empty? ? `tasklist /FO CSV /NH 2>NUL`.to_s : w }
           else
             `ps -A -o comm= 2>/dev/null`.to_s
           end
    found = []
    found << :warlock if list =~ /warlock/i
    found << :wrayth if list =~ /wrayth/i
    found
  rescue StandardError
    []
  end

  def self.valid_name?(n)
    n =~ /\A[A-Z][a-z]{1,15}\z/ && !NOT_NAMES.include?(n)
  end

  # Pull player names out of WHO lines: a name line is one where every word looks like a name.
  # Staff on duty from a plain WHO: {'gamehost' => [...], 'gamemaster' => [...]}. The titles are
  # matched in any capitalization and with any prefix (Senior GameHost, Lead Gamemaster, ...).
  def self.staff_from(lines)
    found = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }
    lines.each do |line|
      line.scan(/\bgame\s*(host|master)\s+([A-Za-z]{2,16})\b/i) do |kind, name|
        n = name.capitalize
        found["game#{kind.downcase}"] << n if valid_name?(n)
      end
    end
    found.transform_values(&:uniq)
  end

  def self.names_from(lines)
    lines.flat_map do |line|
      words = line.strip.split(/\s+/)
      next [] if words.empty? || line.include?(':')
      next [] unless words.all? { |w| w =~ /\A[A-Z][a-z]{1,15}\z/ }
      words.select { |w| valid_name?(w) }
    end.uniq
  end
end

# ================================================================ setup window
# A color wheel (click or drag to pick hue and strength, slider for brightness), plus a
# code box, a swatch and an example line for every profession. Needs Lich's GTK.
module WhoHL
  module HSV
    module_function

    def to_rgb(h, s, v)
      i = (h * 6).floor % 6
      f = h * 6 - (h * 6).floor
      p = v * (1 - s); q = v * (1 - f * s); t = v * (1 - (1 - f) * s)
      [[v, t, p], [q, v, p], [p, v, t], [p, q, v], [t, p, v], [v, p, q]][i]
    end

    def from_rgb(r, g, b)
      max = [r, g, b].max; min = [r, g, b].min; d = max - min
      h = if d.zero? then 0.0
          elsif max == r then ((g - b) / d) % 6
          elsif max == g then (b - r) / d + 2
          else (r - g) / d + 4
          end / 6.0
      [h, max.zero? ? 0.0 : d / max, max]
    end

    def to_hex(h, s, v)
      '#' + to_rgb(h, s, v).map { |c| format('%02x', (c * 255).round.clamp(0, 255)) }.join
    end

    def from_hex(hex)
      from_rgb(*hex.delete('#')[-6..].scan(/../).map { |c| c.to_i(16) / 255.0 })
    end
  end

  # A small pop-up color wheel: click or drag for hue and strength, slider for brightness.
  # Returns '#aarrggbb', 'none' (if allowed) or nil for Cancel. Keeps the code's opacity.
  class WheelDialog
    SIZE = 220

    def self.pick(parent, title, color, allow_none: true)
      new(parent, title, color, allow_none).run
    end

    def initialize(parent, title, color, allow_none)
      @alpha = color.to_s =~ /\A#[0-9a-f]{8}\z/i ? color[1, 2] : 'ff'
      @hsv = color.to_s =~ /\A#[0-9a-f]{6,8}\z/i ? HSV.from_hex(color) : [0.6, 0.6, 0.8]
      @dialog = Gtk::Dialog.new(title: title, parent: parent, flags: [:modal, :destroy_with_parent])
      @dialog.add_button('None', 1) if allow_none
      @dialog.add_button('Cancel', Gtk::ResponseType::CANCEL)
      @dialog.add_button('OK', Gtk::ResponseType::OK)
      box = @dialog.content_area
      box.spacing = 8
      box.border_width = 10
      @code = Gtk::Label.new
      box.pack_start(@code, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      @wheel = Gtk::DrawingArea.new
      @wheel.set_size_request(SIZE, SIZE)
      @wheel.add_events([:button_press_mask, :button1_motion_mask])
      @wheel.signal_connect('draw') { |_, cr| draw(cr); false }
      pick = lambda do |_, ev|
        c = SIZE / 2.0; r = c - 4; dx = ev.x - c; dy = ev.y - c
        @hsv = [(Math.atan2(dy, dx) / (2 * Math::PI)) % 1.0, [Math.hypot(dx, dy) / r, 1.0].min, @hsv[2]]
        changed
        true
      end
      @wheel.signal_connect('button-press-event', &pick)
      @wheel.signal_connect('motion-notify-event', &pick)
      box.pack_start(@wheel, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      @bright = Gtk::Scale.new(:horizontal, 0, 100, 1)
      @bright.draw_value = false
      @bright.value = (@hsv[2] * 100).round
      @bright.signal_connect('value-changed') { |sc| @hsv = [@hsv[0], @hsv[1], sc.value / 100.0]; changed }
      box.pack_start(Gtk::Label.new('Brightness'), expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      box.pack_start(@bright, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      changed
    end

    def current
      "##{@alpha}#{HSV.to_hex(*@hsv)[1..]}"
    end

    def changed
      @code.set_markup("<span font_family='monospace'>#{current}</span>")
      @wheel.queue_draw
    end

    def run
      @dialog.show_all
      res = @dialog.run
      out = case res
            when Gtk::ResponseType::OK then current
            when 1 then 'none'
            end
      @dialog.destroy
      out
    end

    def draw(cr)
      c = SIZE / 2.0; r = c - 4
      360.times do |deg|
        grad = Cairo::RadialPattern.new(c, c, 0, c, c, r)
        grad.add_color_stop_rgb(0, 1, 1, 1)
        grad.add_color_stop_rgb(1, *HSV.to_rgb(deg / 360.0, 1, 1))
        cr.set_source(grad)
        cr.move_to(c, c); cr.arc(c, c, r, deg * Math::PI / 180, (deg + 1.5) * Math::PI / 180); cr.close_path; cr.fill
      end
      cr.set_source_rgba(0, 0, 0, 1 - @hsv[2]); cr.arc(c, c, r, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.fill
      mx = c + Math.cos(@hsv[0] * 2 * Math::PI) * @hsv[1] * r
      my = c + Math.sin(@hsv[0] * 2 * Math::PI) * @hsv[1] * r
      cr.set_line_width(2)
      cr.set_source_rgb(0, 0, 0); cr.arc(mx, my, 7, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.stroke
      cr.set_source_rgb(1, 1, 1); cr.arc(mx, my, 5, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.stroke
    end
  end

  # The "Spell circles" tab: pick a Warlock character, set each circle's bar, background and text
  # colors (code box, or click the swatch for a wheel), see an example bar, save for that character
  # and optionally apply to its Warlock file.
  #   characters: [[char_id, label, folder], ...]   current: char_id or nil
  #   load: ->(char_id) { styles }                  save: ->(char_id, styles, apply) { message }
  class SpellTab
    PARTS = [%w[bar Bar], %w[bg Background], %w[text Text]].freeze
    SAMPLES = { 'minor_spiritual' => 'Spirit Warding I', 'major_spiritual' => 'Spirit Shield', 'cleric' => 'Prayer of Protection',
                'minor_elemental' => 'Elemental Defense I', 'major_elemental' => 'Rapid Fire', 'ranger' => 'Natural Colors',
                'sorcerer' => 'Cloak of Shadows', 'wizard' => 'Haste', 'bard' => 'Sonic Shield', 'empath' => 'Troll\'s Blood',
                'minor_mental' => 'Foresight', 'paladin' => 'Zealot', 'arcane' => 'Mass Blur', 'society' => 'Sigil of Defense' }.freeze
    DEFAULT_BG = '#2b2b2b'
    DEFAULT_BAR = '#4a6fa5'
    DEFAULT_TEXT = '#ffffff'

    attr_reader :widget

    def initialize(parent_window, characters, current, load:, save:)
      @parent = parent_window
      @characters = characters
      @load = load
      @save = save
      @rows = {}
      @dirty = false
      @switch_warned = false
      @syncing = false
      @widget = Gtk::Box.new(:vertical, 8)
      if characters.empty?
        l = Gtk::Label.new('No Warlock characters found yet. Log a character in with Warlock once, then reopen setup.')
        @widget.pack_start(l, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
        return
      end
      @char = characters.find { |c| c[0] == current }&.first || characters.first[0]
      build
      load_char(@char)
    end

    def dirty?
      @dirty
    end

    private

    def build
      top = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 8)
      top.pack_start(Gtk::Label.new('Character:'), expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      @combo = Gtk::ComboBoxText.new
      @characters.each { |_, label, _| @combo.append_text(label) }
      @combo.active = @characters.index { |c| c[0] == @char } || 0
      @combo.signal_connect('changed') { switch_char }
      top.pack_start(@combo, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      hint = Gtk::Label.new('Each character has its own colors. Bars use these in Active Spells, Buffs, Debuffs and Cooldowns.')
      hint.xalign = 0
      top.pack_start(hint, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 6)
      @widget.pack_start(top, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      grid = Gtk::Grid.new
      grid.row_spacing = 4
      grid.column_spacing = 8
      ['Circle', 'Bar', '', 'Background', '', 'Text', '', 'Example'].each_with_index do |h, i|
        l = Gtk::Label.new
        l.set_markup("<b>#{h}</b>")
        l.xalign = 0
        grid.attach(l, i, 0, 1, 1)
      end
      SpellCircles::CIRCLES.each_with_index do |(key, label, _), i|
        row = {}
        l = Gtk::Label.new(label)
        l.xalign = 0
        grid.attach(l, 0, i + 1, 1, 1)
        col = 1
        PARTS.each do |part, plabel|
          entry = Gtk::Entry.new
          entry.width_chars = 10
          entry.max_length = 9
          entry.tooltip_text = "#{plabel}: #AARRGGBB (Warlock) or #RRGGBB, or none for Warlock's default"
          entry.signal_connect('changed') { |e| typed(key, part, e.text) }
          button = Gtk::Button.new
          swatch = Gtk::DrawingArea.new
          swatch.set_size_request(26, 18)
          swatch.signal_connect('draw') { |_, cr| draw_swatch(cr, @styles[key][part]); false }
          button.add(swatch)
          button.tooltip_text = "Pick the #{plabel.downcase} color on a wheel"
          button.signal_connect('clicked') do
            c = WheelDialog.pick(@parent, "#{label} #{plabel.downcase}", @styles[key][part])
            set_color(key, part, c) if c
          end
          grid.attach(entry, col, i + 1, 1, 1)
          grid.attach(button, col + 1, i + 1, 1, 1)
          row[part] = entry
          row["#{part}_swatch"] = swatch
          col += 2
        end
        example = Gtk::DrawingArea.new
        example.set_size_request(230, 18)
        example.signal_connect('draw') { |_, cr| draw_example(cr, key); false }
        grid.attach(example, 7, i + 1, 1, 1)
        row[:example] = example
        @rows[key] = row
      end
      scroll = Gtk::ScrolledWindow.new
      scroll.set_policy(:never, :automatic)
      scroll.add(grid)
      @widget.pack_start(scroll, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)

      @status = Gtk::Label.new('')
      @status.xalign = 0
      @status.ellipsize = :end
      buttons = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 8)
      buttons.pack_start(@status, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
      defaults = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Use defaults')
      defaults.tooltip_text = 'Put every circle back to the default colors (not saved until you save)'
      defaults.signal_connect('clicked') do
        SpellCircles.defaults.each { |k, st| st.each { |part, c| set_color(k, part, c) } }
      end
      save = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Save colors')
      save.tooltip_text = "Remember these colors for this character"
      save.signal_connect('clicked') { save!(false) }
      apply = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Save + apply to Warlock')
      apply.tooltip_text = "Remember these colors and color this character's spell bars in Warlock now"
      apply.signal_connect('clicked') { save!(true) }
      [defaults, save, apply].each { |b| buttons.pack_start(b, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0) }
      @widget.pack_start(buttons, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
    end

    def char_label
      @characters.find { |c| c[0] == @char }&.at(1) || @char
    end

    def load_char(char_id)
      @char = char_id
      @styles = @load.call(char_id).to_h { |k, st| [k, st.dup] }
      @syncing = true
      @rows.each { |k, row| PARTS.each { |part, _| row[part].text = @styles[k][part] } }
      @syncing = false
      @rows.each_value { |row| redraw(row) }
      @dirty = false
      @status.text = "Colors for #{char_label}."
    rescue StandardError => e
      @status.text = "ERROR: #{e.message}"
    end

    # Switching characters with unsaved changes takes a second try, like closing does.
    def switch_char
      return if @syncing
      wanted = @characters[@combo.active]&.first
      return if wanted.nil? || wanted == @char
      if @dirty && !@switch_warned
        @switch_warned = true
        @status.set_markup("<span foreground='#cc0000'>Unsaved changes for #{char_label}. Save them, or pick #{@characters[@combo.active][1]} again to throw them away.</span>")
        @syncing = true
        @combo.active = @characters.index { |c| c[0] == @char }
        @syncing = false
        return
      end
      @switch_warned = false
      load_char(wanted)
    end

    def typed(key, part, text)
      return if @syncing
      t = text.strip.downcase
      t = 'none' if t.empty?
      c = Colors.canon(t)
      if c
        @styles[key][part] = c
        changed(key)
      else
        @status.text = "#{SpellCircles.label(key)} #{part}: use #AARRGGBB, #RRGGBB or none"
      end
    end

    def set_color(key, part, color)
      @styles[key][part] = color
      @syncing = true
      @rows[key][part].text = color
      @syncing = false
      changed(key)
    end

    def changed(key)
      @dirty = true
      @switch_warned = false
      redraw(@rows[key])
      @status.text = "#{SpellCircles.label(key)} changed for #{char_label} (not saved yet)"
    end

    def redraw(row)
      PARTS.each { |part, _| row["#{part}_swatch"].queue_draw }
      row[:example].queue_draw
    end

    def rgba(color, fallback)
      c = color == 'none' ? "#ff#{fallback[1..]}" : Colors.canon(color) || "#ff#{fallback[1..]}"
      a = c[1, 2].to_i(16) / 255.0
      HSV.to_rgb(*HSV.from_hex(c)) + [a]
    end

    def draw_swatch(cr, color)
      if color == 'none'
        cr.set_source_rgb(1, 1, 1); cr.rectangle(0, 0, 26, 18); cr.fill
        cr.set_source_rgb(0.8, 0.1, 0.1); cr.set_line_width(2); cr.move_to(2, 16); cr.line_to(24, 2); cr.stroke
      else
        cr.set_source_rgba(*rgba(color, '#000000')); cr.rectangle(0, 0, 26, 18); cr.fill
      end
    end

    # What the bar looks like in Warlock: background, bar about two-thirds full, spell name on top.
    def draw_example(cr, key)
      st = @styles[key]
      cr.set_source_rgba(*rgba(st['bg'], DEFAULT_BG)); cr.rectangle(0, 0, 230, 18); cr.fill
      cr.set_source_rgba(*rgba(st['bar'], DEFAULT_BAR)); cr.rectangle(0, 0, 150, 18); cr.fill
      if st['text'] == 'none'
        # Warlock picks the text color itself here; show whichever of black or white reads on the bar.
        r, g, b, = rgba(st['bar'], DEFAULT_BAR)
        cr.set_source_rgb(*(0.299 * r + 0.587 * g + 0.114 * b > 0.6 ? [0, 0, 0] : [1, 1, 1]))
      else
        cr.set_source_rgba(*rgba(st['text'], DEFAULT_TEXT))
      end
      cr.select_font_face('Sans', Cairo::FONT_SLANT_NORMAL, Cairo::FONT_WEIGHT_NORMAL)
      cr.set_font_size(12)
      cr.move_to(6, 13)
      cr.show_text(SAMPLES[key] || SpellCircles.label(key))
    end

    def save!(apply)
      bad = @rows.flat_map { |k, row| PARTS.filter_map { |part, pl| "#{SpellCircles.label(k)} #{pl.downcase}" unless Colors.canon(row[part].text.strip.empty? ? 'none' : row[part].text.strip.downcase) } }
      raise Error, "Fix #{bad.join(', ')} (#AARRGGBB, #RRGGBB or none). Nothing saved." unless bad.empty?
      @status.text = @save.call(@char, @styles.to_h { |k, st| [k, st.dup] }, apply).to_s
      @dirty = false
    rescue StandardError => e
      @status.set_markup("<span foreground='#cc0000'>ERROR: #{e.message.gsub('&', '&amp;').gsub('<', '&lt;').gsub('>', '&gt;')}</span>")
    end
  end

  class SetupWindow
    EXAMPLE_BG   = '#1e1e1e'   # dark, like a game window
    EXAMPLE_TEXT = '#c8c8c8'
    WHEEL        = 240
    PARTS        = { 'text' => 'Text', 'bg' => 'Background' }.freeze
    SAMPLE_NAMES = { 'bard' => 'Lyra', 'cleric' => 'Ondrel', 'empath' => 'Mirelle', 'monk' => 'Kesh',
                     'paladin' => 'Thoran', 'ranger' => 'Faelan', 'rogue' => 'Vex', 'sorcerer' => 'Morvaine',
                     'warrior' => 'Brakka', 'wizard' => 'Alba' , 'gamehost' => 'Myerz', 'gamemaster' => 'Halvard' }.freeze

    # styles: {profession => {'text' => '#rrggbb', 'bg' => '#rrggbb' or 'none'}}
    # on_save: called with the edited styles; returns a status message.
    # spells: optional { characters:, current:, load:, save: } for the Spell circles tab (see SpellTab).
    def initialize(styles, on_save:, on_close: nil, spells: nil)
      @spells = spells
      @styles = styles.to_h { |p, st| [p, st.dup] }
      @on_save = on_save
      @on_close = on_close
      @rows = {}
      @selected = CATEGORIES.first
      @part = 'text'
      @syncing = false
      @dirty = false
      @close_warned = false
      @hsv = hsv_for(current)
      build
    end

    attr_reader :window

    private

    def current
      @styles[@selected][@part]
    end

    # Where the wheel starts for a color; "none" starts at a dark blue so a click gives a usable background.
    def hsv_for(color)
      color == 'none' ? [0.62, 0.6, 0.25] : HSV.from_hex(color)
    end

    def build
      @window = Gtk::Window.new('highlights - colors')
      @window.set_default_size(1000, 700)
      @window.border_width = 12
      @window.signal_connect('destroy') { @on_close&.call }

      outer = Gtk::Box.new(:vertical, 10)
      names_page = Gtk::Box.new(:vertical, 10)
      names_page.border_width = 6
      main = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 18)
      names_page.pack_start(main, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
      main.pack_start(build_list, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
      main.pack_start(build_wheel, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      @status = Gtk::Label.new('Pick a profession and Text or Background, then click the wheel or paste a code from Warlock (#ff5599ff) or Wrayth (#5599ff).')
      @status.xalign = 0
      @status.ellipsize = :end
      buttons = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 8)
      buttons.pack_start(@status, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
      reset = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Reset profession')
      reset.tooltip_text = 'Put this profession back to its default text color and no background'
      reset.signal_connect('clicked') do
        apply(@selected, 'text', DEFAULT_COLORS[@selected], from: :button)
        apply(@selected, 'bg', 'none', from: :button)
      end
      save = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Save colors')
      save.tooltip_text = 'Remember these colors for next time and for new scans'
      save.signal_connect('clicked') { save!(recolor: false) }
      save_all = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Save + recolor')
      save_all.tooltip_text = 'Remember these colors and also recolor the names already saved in the front end you are using'
      save_all.signal_connect('clicked') { save!(recolor: true) }
      [reset, save, save_all].each { |b| buttons.pack_start(b, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0) }
      names_page.pack_start(buttons, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      notebook = Gtk::Notebook.new
      notebook.append_page(names_page, Gtk::Label.new('Names'))
      if @spells
        @spell_tab = SpellTab.new(@window, @spells[:characters], @spells[:current], load: @spells[:load], save: @spells[:save])
        @spell_tab.widget.border_width = 6
        notebook.append_page(@spell_tab.widget, Gtk::Label.new('Spell circles'))
      end
      outer.pack_start(notebook, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)

      bottom = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 8)
      @close_status = Gtk::Label.new('')
      @close_status.xalign = 0
      bottom.pack_start(@close_status, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
      close = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'Close')
      close.signal_connect('clicked') { @window.destroy if ok_to_close? }
      @window.signal_connect('delete-event') { !ok_to_close? }    # true = keep the window open
      bottom.pack_start(close, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      outer.pack_start(bottom, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      @window.add(outer)
      sync_wheel
      @window.show_all
    end

    def build_list
      grid = Gtk::Grid.new
      grid.row_spacing = 6
      grid.column_spacing = 8
      [['Profession', 0], ['Text', 1], ['Background', 3], ['Example', 5]].each do |h, col|
        l = Gtk::Label.new
        l.set_markup("<b>#{h}</b>")
        l.xalign = 0
        grid.attach(l, col, 0, col.between?(1, 4) ? 2 : 1, 1)
      end
      group = nil
      CATEGORIES.each_with_index do |p, i|
        radio = group ? Gtk::RadioButton.new(member: group, label: WhoHL.label(p)) : Gtk::RadioButton.new(label: WhoHL.label(p))
        group ||= radio
        radio.signal_connect('toggled') { |r| select(p, @part) if r.active? }
        row = { radio: radio }

        col = 1
        PARTS.each_key do |part|
          entry = Gtk::Entry.new
          entry.width_chars = 10
          entry.max_length = 9
          entry.text = @styles[p][part]
          entry.tooltip_text = '#AARRGGBB (Warlock) or #RRGGBB (Wrayth)' + (part == 'bg' ? ', or none for no background' : '')
          entry.signal_connect('changed') { |e| typed(p, part, e.text) }
          entry.signal_connect('focus-in-event') do
            radio.active = true
            select(p, part)
            false
          end

          swatch = Gtk::DrawingArea.new
          swatch.set_size_request(28, 22)
          swatch.signal_connect('draw') { |_, cr| draw_swatch(cr, @styles[p][part]); false }

          grid.attach(entry, col, i + 1, 1, 1)
          grid.attach(swatch, col + 1, i + 1, 1, 1)
          row[part] = entry
          row["#{part}_swatch"] = swatch
          col += 2
        end

        example = Gtk::Label.new
        example.xalign = 0
        example.margin_start = 6
        example.margin_end = 6
        box = Gtk::EventBox.new
        box.override_background_color(:normal, Gdk::RGBA.parse(EXAMPLE_BG))
        box.add(example)
        row[:example] = example

        grid.attach(radio, 0, i + 1, 1, 1)
        grid.attach(box, 5, i + 1, 1, 1)
        @rows[p] = row
        update_example(p)
      end
      grid
    end

    def draw_swatch(cr, color)
      if color == 'none'
        cr.set_source_rgb(1, 1, 1); cr.rectangle(0, 0, 28, 22); cr.fill
        cr.set_source_rgb(0.8, 0.1, 0.1); cr.set_line_width(2)
        cr.move_to(2, 20); cr.line_to(26, 2); cr.stroke      # "none" slash
      else
        cr.set_source_rgb(*HSV.to_rgb(*HSV.from_hex(color)))
        cr.rectangle(0, 0, 28, 22); cr.fill
      end
      cr.set_line_width(1)
      cr.set_source_rgb(0.4, 0.4, 0.4); cr.rectangle(0.5, 0.5, 27, 21); cr.stroke
    end

    def build_wheel
      box = Gtk::Box.new(:vertical, 8)
      @picked = Gtk::Label.new
      box.pack_start(@picked, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      switch = Gtk::Box.new(:horizontal, 0)
      switch.style_context.add_class('linked')
      @part_buttons = {}
      first = nil
      PARTS.each do |part, label|
        b = first ? Gtk::RadioButton.new(member: first, label: label) : Gtk::RadioButton.new(label: label)
        first ||= b
        b.mode = false                      # draw as toggle buttons, not dots
        b.signal_connect('toggled') { |r| select(@selected, part) if r.active? && !@syncing }
        switch.pack_start(b, expand: true, fill: true, padding: 0)
        @part_buttons[part] = b
      end
      box.pack_start(switch, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      @wheel = Gtk::DrawingArea.new
      @wheel.set_size_request(WHEEL, WHEEL)
      @wheel.add_events([:button_press_mask, :button1_motion_mask])
      @wheel.signal_connect('draw') { |_, cr| draw_wheel(cr); false }
      pick = lambda do |_, ev|
        c = WHEEL / 2.0; r = c - 4
        dx = ev.x - c; dy = ev.y - c
        @hsv = [(Math.atan2(dy, dx) / (2 * Math::PI)) % 1.0, [Math.hypot(dx, dy) / r, 1.0].min, @hsv[2]]
        apply(@selected, @part, from_wheel, from: :wheel)
        true
      end
      @wheel.signal_connect('button-press-event', &pick)
      @wheel.signal_connect('motion-notify-event', &pick)
      box.pack_start(@wheel, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      box.pack_start(Gtk::Label.new('Brightness'), expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      @bright = Gtk::Scale.new(:horizontal, 0, 100, 1)
      @bright.draw_value = false
      @bright.signal_connect('value-changed') do |s|
        next if @syncing
        @hsv = [@hsv[0], @hsv[1], s.value / 100.0]
        apply(@selected, @part, from_wheel, from: :wheel)
      end
      box.pack_start(@bright, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)

      @none = Gtk::Button.new(label: 'No background')
      @none.signal_connect('clicked') { apply(@selected, 'bg', 'none', from: :button) }
      box.pack_start(@none, expand: false, fill: false, padding: 0)
      box
    end

    def draw_wheel(cr)
      c = WHEEL / 2.0; r = c - 4
      360.times do |deg|
        a1 = deg * Math::PI / 180; a2 = (deg + 1.5) * Math::PI / 180
        grad = Cairo::RadialPattern.new(c, c, 0, c, c, r)
        grad.add_color_stop_rgb(0, 1, 1, 1)
        grad.add_color_stop_rgb(1, *HSV.to_rgb(deg / 360.0, 1, 1))
        cr.set_source(grad)
        cr.move_to(c, c); cr.arc(c, c, r, a1, a2); cr.close_path; cr.fill
      end
      cr.set_source_rgba(0, 0, 0, 1 - @hsv[2])        # darken for brightness
      cr.arc(c, c, r, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.fill
      return if current == 'none'                     # no marker: nothing picked yet
      mx = c + Math.cos(@hsv[0] * 2 * Math::PI) * @hsv[1] * r
      my = c + Math.sin(@hsv[0] * 2 * Math::PI) * @hsv[1] * r
      cr.set_line_width(2)
      cr.set_source_rgb(0, 0, 0); cr.arc(mx, my, 7, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.stroke
      cr.set_source_rgb(1, 1, 1); cr.arc(mx, my, 5, 0, 2 * Math::PI); cr.stroke
    end

    # The wheel's color as a Warlock code, keeping the opacity the code already had.
    def from_wheel
      alpha = current == 'none' ? 'ff' : current[1, 2]
      "##{alpha}#{HSV.to_hex(*@hsv)[1..]}"
    end

    def select(p, part)
      @selected = p
      @part = part
      @hsv = hsv_for(current)
      sync_wheel
    end

    # Text typed in a code box. Applied when it's a valid code; otherwise just a hint.
    def typed(p, part, text)
      return if @syncing
      t = read_code(part, text)
      if t
        apply(p, part, t, from: :entry)
      else
        @status.text = "#{WhoHL.label(p)} #{PARTS[part].downcase}: use #AARRGGBB (Warlock) or #RRGGBB (Wrayth)" +
                       (part == 'bg' ? ', or none' : '')
      end
    end

    # A code box's text as a Warlock code ('#aarrggbb' or 'none'), or nil if it isn't one.
    def read_code(part, text)
      t = text.strip.downcase
      t = 'none' if part == 'bg' && t.empty?
      return nil if t == 'none' && part != 'bg'
      Colors.canon(t)
    end

    # One place that changes a color, wherever the change came from.
    def apply(p, part, value, from:)
      @styles[p][part] = value
      row = @rows[p]
      if from != :entry
        @syncing = true
        row[part].text = value
        @syncing = false
      end
      row["#{part}_swatch"].queue_draw
      update_example(p)
      if p == @selected && part == @part
        @hsv = hsv_for(value) unless from == :wheel
        sync_wheel
      end
      @dirty = true
      @close_warned = false
      @status.text = "#{WhoHL.label(p)} #{PARTS[part].downcase}: #{value} (not saved yet)"
    end

    def sync_wheel
      @syncing = true
      @part_buttons[@part].active = true
      @bright.value = (@hsv[2] * 100).round
      @syncing = false
      @none.sensitive = @part == 'bg'
      @wheel.queue_draw
      @picked.set_markup("<b>#{WhoHL.label(@selected)}</b> #{PARTS[@part].downcase}:  #{current}")
    end

    def update_example(p)
      st = @styles[p]
      name_attrs = "foreground='#{Colors.rgb(st['text'])}'"
      name_attrs += " background='#{Colors.rgb(st['bg'])}'" unless st['bg'] == 'none'
      name = "<span #{name_attrs}>#{SAMPLE_NAMES[p] || 'Someone'}</span>"
      line = STAFF.include?(p) ? "#{WhoHL.label(p)} #{name} just arrived." : "#{name} the #{p} just arrived."
      @rows[p][:example].set_markup("<span font_family='monospace' foreground='#{EXAMPLE_TEXT}'>#{line}</span>")
    end

    # Close only when everything is saved, or on a second Close after a warning.
    def ok_to_close?
      dirty = []
      dirty << 'Names' if @dirty
      dirty << 'Spell circles' if @spell_tab&.dirty?
      return true if dirty.empty? || @close_warned
      @close_warned = true
      @close_status.set_markup("<span foreground='#cc0000'>Unsaved changes on #{dirty.join(' and ')}. Save them, or click Close again to throw them away.</span>")
      false
    end

    def save!(recolor:)
      bad = @rows.flat_map do |p, r|
        PARTS.filter_map do |part, label|
          "#{WhoHL.label(p)} #{label.downcase}" unless read_code(part, r[part].text)
        end
      end
      raise Error, "Fix #{bad.join(', ')} (use #AARRGGBB like Warlock or #RRGGBB like Wrayth; background can be none). Nothing saved." unless bad.empty?
      @status.text = @on_save.call(@styles.to_h { |p, st| [p, st.dup] }, recolor).to_s
      @dirty = false
    rescue StandardError => e
      @status.set_markup("<span foreground='#cc0000'>ERROR: #{e.message.gsub('&', '&amp;').gsub('<', '&lt;').gsub('>', '&gt;')}</span>")
    end
  end
end

# ====================================================================== Lich side
if defined?(Script) && defined?(put)
  ME = (script.name rescue 'highlights').to_s

  def hl_msg(text)
    text.to_s.each_line { |l| respond "[#{ME}] #{l.chomp}" }
  end

  # Runs a WHO command and returns its lines (until "Use WHO HELP", or it goes quiet).
  def hl_who(command)
    clear
    put command
    lines = []
    names_seen = false
    last = Time.now
    deadline = Time.now + 15
    loop do
      line = get?
      if line.nil?
        break if names_seen && Time.now - last > 1.5
        break if !names_seen && Time.now - last > 4
        break if Time.now > deadline
        sleep 0.05
        next
      end
      last = Time.now
      lines << line
      names_seen ||= !WhoHL.names_from([line]).empty? || !WhoHL.staff_from([line]).empty?
      break if line =~ /Use WHO HELP|^\s*Total\b|There (?:is|are) no/i
    end
    lines
  end

  args = script.vars[1..] || []
  opts = {}
  args = args.reject do |a|
    case a
    when /\A--global\z/i then opts[:global] = true
    when /\A--me\z/i     then opts[:me] = true      # the default; still accepted
    when /\A--fresh\z/i  then opts[:fresh] = true
    when /\A--replace\z/i then opts[:replace] = true
    when /\A--dry\z/i    then opts[:dry] = true
    when /\A--dir=(.+)\z/i then opts[:dir] = $1.delete('"')
    when /\A--fe=(warlock|wrayth|saga)\z/i then opts[:fe] = $1
    when /\A--fe/i then opts[:bad_fe] = a
    when /\A#(?:[0-9a-f]{6}|[0-9a-f]{8})\z/i then opts[:color] = a
    when /\A--bg=(#?(?:[0-9a-f]{6}|[0-9a-f]{8})|none)\z/i then opts[:bg] = $1
    when /\A--bg/i then opts[:bad_bg] = a
    else next false
    end
    true
  end
  cmd = (args.shift || '').downcase

  # The text + background colors for a profession: what was typed on the command line (and
  # remembered), else what was picked in setup, else the default.
  pick_color = lambda do |p|
    if opts[:color] || opts[:bg]
      WhoHL::Colors.set(p, text: opts[:color], bg: opts[:bg])
    else
      WhoHL::Colors.get(p)
    end
  end
  skipped_note = lambda do |names, list|
    "Left alone (you already have your own #{list} entr#{names.size == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'}): #{names.join(', ')}"
  end
  conflict_notes = lambda do |conflicts, p|
    next [] if conflicts.nil? || conflicts.empty?
    lines = ["#{conflicts.size == 1 ? 'This person is' : 'These people are'} saved under another profession but " \
             "now #{cmd == 'add' ? 'being added as' : 'listed as'} #{p}; nothing was changed for them:"]
    conflicts.sort.each { |n, q| lines << "  #{n}: saved as #{q}. To move: ;#{ME} drop #{q} #{n}   then   ;#{ME} add #{p} #{n}" }
    lines
  end
  prof_ok = lambda do |p|
    raise WhoHL::Error, "Savant isn't a playable profession yet." if p == 'savant'
    WhoHL::CATEGORIES.include?(p) or raise WhoHL::Error, "Unknown profession #{p.inspect}. Use one of: #{WhoHL::CATEGORIES.join(' ')}"
  end

  begin
    raise WhoHL::Error, "Use --bg=#AARRGGBB, --bg=#RRGGBB or --bg=none (got #{opts[:bad_bg]})" if opts[:bad_bg]
    raise WhoHL::Error, "Use --fe=warlock, --fe=wrayth or --fe=saga (got #{opts[:bad_fe]})" if opts[:bad_fe]

    if %w[help].include?(cmd) || cmd.empty?
      hl_msg "Usage: ;#{ME} <profession|all> [#AARRGGBB] [--bg=#AARRGGBB|none] [--fresh] [--dry]"
      hl_msg "       ;#{ME} add|drop <profession> Name ...   ;#{ME} remove <profession>   ;#{ME} list   ;#{ME} where"
      hl_msg "       ;#{ME} setup   (pick each profession's text and background colors)"
      hl_msg "       ;#{ME} reapply (put saved names back)   ;#{ME} watch (Wrayth: keep them there while you play)"
      hl_msg "       ;#{ME} cleanup (Wrayth: remove blank entries from the names list)"
      hl_msg "       ;#{ME} spells [--replace|off|list]  (Warlock: color spell bars by circle, per character)"
      hl_msg "Warlock only: --global saves for all characters. Professions: #{WhoHL::PROFESSIONS.join(' ')}"
      hl_msg "Staff (from WHO's Staff on duty): gamehost gamemaster, or staff for both. all scans everything."
      exit
    end

    fe = WhoHL.frontend(opts[:fe])

    # Spell bar colors are a Warlock feature; these work from any front end if Warlock is set up.
    spell_numbers = (Spell.list.map { |sp| sp.num.to_i } rescue nil)
    warlock_dir = (WhoHL::Warlock.config_dir(opts[:dir]) rescue nil)
    spell_load = lambda do |char_id|
      WhoHL::SpellCircles.saved(char_id) || begin
        folder = WhoHL.warlock_characters(warlock_dir).find { |c| c[0] == char_id }&.at(2)
        folder ? WhoHL::SpellCircles.seed(WhoHL::ProgressBarsFile.new(File.join(folder, 'progressbars.toml'))) : WhoHL::SpellCircles.defaults
      end
    end
    spell_report = lambda do |r, label|
      msg = r[:removed] > 0 ? "Took this script's colors off #{r[:removed]} spell bar#{r[:removed] == 1 ? '' : 's'} for #{label} (bars you had colored yourself got your colors back)." :
                              "Spell bars for #{label}: #{r[:written]} colored, #{r[:adopted]} you'd already colored to match (now kept in step)."
      msg += " Left alone (you colored them differently; --replace to overwrite): #{r[:kept].join(', ')}." unless r[:kept].empty?
      msg
    end

    # ---- one set of operations per front end
    if fe == :warlock
      dir = WhoHL::Warlock.config_dir(opts[:dir])
      moved = WhoHL.migrate_all(dir)
      if moved > 0
        hl_msg "Moved #{moved} profession list#{moved == 1 ? '' : 's'} from one-line regex highlights to Warlock's Names list " \
               "(one entry per person; Settings -> Names). The old regex highlights were removed."
      end
      character = opts[:global] ? nil : Char.name
      whom = character ? "#{character} (Warlock)" : 'all characters (Warlock Global)'
      list_name = 'Warlock Names'
      folder = -> { WhoHL::Warlock.target(dir, character)[1] }
      store = ->(&blk) { WhoHL.update(dir: dir, character: character, profession: nil, &blk) }
      reader = -> { WhoHL::NamesFile.new(File.join(folder.(), 'names.toml')) }
      where_text = -> { "#{File.join(folder.(), 'names.toml')} (Warlock: Settings -> Names, #{character || 'Global'})" }
      done_note = "Warlock picks this up on its own: Settings -> Names, #{character || 'Global'}."
      recolor = lambda do |colors|
        n = WhoHL.recolor_all(dir, colors)
        n.zero? ? 'No Warlock names needed recoloring.' : "Recolored names in #{n} Warlock file#{n == 1 ? '' : 's'}."
      end
      check = -> { folder.() }
    elsif fe == :saga
      raise WhoHL::Error, "--global is for Warlock; Saga keeps highlights per character." if opts[:global]
      game = (XMLData.game rescue nil)
      key = WhoHL::Saga.character_key(WhoHL::Saga.read_prefs, Char.name, game)
      whom = "#{Char.name} (Saga)"
      list_name = 'Saga highlights'
      import_file = WhoHL::Saga.import_path(key)
      store = lambda do |&blk|
        path, res, = WhoHL.saga_update(Char.name, game, &blk)
        [path, res]
      end
      reader = -> { WhoHL::SagaFile.new(WhoHL::Saga.read_prefs, key, WhoHL::Saga.load_owned(key)) }
      where_text = -> { "#{import_file} (Saga: Menu -> Import Settings; your current highlights come from #{WhoHL::Saga.prefs_path})" }
      done_note = "Saga import file ready: #{import_file}\n" \
                  "In Saga: Menu -> Import Settings, pick that file, choose 'Clear and overwrite' (not Keep and merge). It holds only highlights (your current " \
                  "ones plus the scanned names), so layout, macros and other settings aren't touched. Import it before you " \
                  "change any highlight in Saga, or rebuild it first with ;#{ME} reapply, so those changes aren't undone."
      recolor = lambda do |colors|
        _, n = store.() { |f| colors.count { |p, c| f.recolor(p, c) } }
        n.zero? ? 'No Saga classes needed recoloring.' : "Recolored #{n} class#{n == 1 ? '' : 'es'}. #{done_note}"
      end
      check = -> { reader.() }
    else
      raise WhoHL::Error, "--global is for Warlock; Wrayth keeps names per character." if opts[:global]
      pid = (XMLData.player_id rescue nil)
      game = (XMLData.game rescue nil)
      whom = "#{Char.name} (Wrayth)"
      list_name = 'Wrayth names'
      store = lambda do |&blk|
        path, res, file = WhoHL.wrayth_update(pid, game, &blk)
        @hl_last_file = file
        if file.blanks_removed > 0 || file.compacted
          parts = []
          parts << "#{file.blanks_removed} blank entr#{file.blanks_removed == 1 ? 'y' : 'ies'}" if file.blanks_removed > 0
          parts << 'the line breaks between entries (Wrayth shows those as blank entries)' if file.compacted
          hl_msg "Fixed Wrayth's names list: removed #{parts.join(' and ')}."
        end
        [path, res]
      end
      reader = -> { f = WhoHL::Wrayth.settings_path(pid, game); WhoHL::WraythFile.new(f, WhoHL::Wrayth.load_owned(f)) }
      where_text = -> { "#{WhoHL::Wrayth.settings_path(pid, game)} (Wrayth: names for #{Char.name})" }
      done_note = "Saved in Wrayth's settings file. Wrayth loads it when it starts, so these show up the next time you log " \
                  "#{Char.name} in. Changing a Wrayth setting before then makes Wrayth save over them: run ;#{ME} reapply " \
                  "afterward, or keep ;#{ME} watch running."
      recolor = lambda do |colors|
        _, n = store.() { |f| colors.count { |p, c| f.recolor(p, c) } }
        n.zero? ? 'No Wrayth names needed recoloring.' : "Recolored #{n} profession#{n == 1 ? '' : 's'} in Wrayth's file (shows at next login)."
      end
      check = -> { WhoHL::Wrayth.settings_path(pid, game) }
    end

    # Which palette colors Wrayth ended up with, when they differ from what was asked for.
    slot_note = lambda do |p|
      next nil unless fe == :wrayth && @hl_last_file && (sl = @hl_last_file.slots[p])
      want = WhoHL::Colors.get(p)
      parts = ["text @#{sl['text'][0]} #{sl['text'][1]}"]
      parts << (sl['bg'] ? "background @#{sl['bg'][0]} #{sl['bg'][1]}" : 'no background')
      "  Wrayth palette colors used for #{p}: #{parts.join(', ')} (closest to #{WhoHL::Colors.rgb(want['text'])}" \
        "#{want['bg'] == 'none' ? '' : " on #{WhoHL::Colors.rgb(want['bg'])}"})"
    end

    case cmd
    when 'setup'
      raise WhoHL::Error, "This Lich has no GTK, so the setup window can't open. Set colors with ;#{ME} <profession> #AARRGGBB" unless defined?(Gtk)
      closed = false
      window = nil
      on_save = lambda do |colors, do_recolor|
        WhoHL::Colors.save(colors)
        msg = "Colors saved to #{WhoHL::Colors.path}."
        msg += ' ' + recolor.(colors) if do_recolor
        hl_msg msg
        msg
      end
      start_colors = WhoHL::Colors.all          # read here, in the script, before the window opens
      spells = nil
      if warlock_dir
        chars = WhoHL.warlock_characters(warlock_dir)
        current = chars.find { |c| c[0].split(':', 2)[1] == WhoHL::Warlock.sanitize(Char.name.downcase) }&.first
        spells = {
          characters: chars, current: current, load: spell_load,
          save: lambda do |char_id, styles, apply|
            previous = spell_load.(char_id)       # what these circles were before this save
            WhoHL::SpellCircles.save(char_id, styles)
            label = chars.find { |c| c[0] == char_id }&.at(1) || char_id
            msg = "Spell colors saved for #{label}."
            if apply
              folder = chars.find { |c| c[0] == char_id }[2]
              msg += ' ' + spell_report.(WhoHL.apply_spell_colors(warlock_dir, char_id, folder, styles, spell_numbers,
                                                                  previous: previous), label)
            end
            hl_msg msg
            msg
          end
        }
      end
      Gtk.queue do
        window = WhoHL::SetupWindow.new(start_colors, on_save: on_save, on_close: -> { closed = true }, spells: spells).window
      end
      before_dying { Gtk.queue { (window.destroy rescue nil) unless closed } }
      hl_msg "Setup window open. Close it when you're done."
      sleep 0.25 until closed

    when 'spells'
      raise WhoHL::Error, "Spell bar colors are for Warlock, and Warlock's settings folder wasn't found." unless warlock_dir
      char_id, folder = WhoHL::Warlock.target(warlock_dir, Char.name)
      label = "#{Char.name} (Warlock)"
      sub = args.shift.to_s.downcase
      if sub == 'off'
        hl_msg spell_report.(WhoHL.apply_spell_colors(warlock_dir, char_id, folder, nil, spell_numbers), label)
      elsif sub == 'list'
        styles = spell_load.(char_id)
        saved = WhoHL::SpellCircles.saved(char_id) ? 'saved' : 'not saved yet (from your current Warlock colors / defaults)'
        hl_msg "Spell circle colors for #{label}, #{saved}:"
        styles.each { |k, st| hl_msg "  #{WhoHL::SpellCircles.label(k).ljust(18)} bar #{st['bar'].ljust(9)}  background #{st['bg'].ljust(9)}  text #{st['text']}" }
      else
        styles = spell_load.(char_id)
        WhoHL::SpellCircles.save(char_id, styles) unless WhoHL::SpellCircles.saved(char_id)
        hl_msg spell_report.(WhoHL.apply_spell_colors(warlock_dir, char_id, folder, styles, spell_numbers,
                                                       replace: opts[:replace], previous: styles), label)
        hl_msg "Warlock picks this up on its own. Change the colors in ;#{ME} setup (Spell circles tab)."
      end

    when 'where'
      hl_msg "Front end: #{fe.to_s.capitalize}. Saving to #{where_text.()}"

    when 'list'
      file = reader.()
      saved = file.saved_professions
      hl_msg(saved.empty? ? "Nothing saved yet for #{whom}." : "Saved for #{whom}:")
      saved.each do |p|
        n = file.names(p); st = file.style(p) || WhoHL::Colors.get(p)
        hl_msg "  #{p.ljust(10)} text #{st['text']}  background #{st['bg'].ljust(9)}  #{n.size} name#{n.size == 1 ? '' : 's'}: #{n.join(', ')}"
      end
      if fe == :wrayth && !(gone = file.missing).empty?
        hl_msg "Missing from Wrayth's file (Wrayth saved over them): #{gone.size}. Run ;#{ME} reapply to put them back."
      end
      if fe == :saga && !(gone = file.missing).empty?
        hl_msg "Not in Saga yet: #{gone.size}. Import #{import_file} in Saga (Menu -> Import Settings); ;#{ME} reapply rebuilds it."
      end

    when 'remove'
      p = args.shift.to_s.downcase; prof_ok.(p)
      path, = store.() { |f| f.set(p, [], nil) }
      hl_msg "Removed the #{p} names from #{path}"
      hl_msg 'Wrayth shows the change at your next login.' if fe == :wrayth
      hl_msg done_note if fe == :saga

    when 'cleanup'
      raise WhoHL::Error, "cleanup is for Wrayth's names list." unless fe == :wrayth
      file = reader.()
      if file.blanks_removed.zero? && !file.compacted
        hl_msg "Wrayth's names list has no blank entries. Nothing to do."
      else
        store.() { nil }
        hl_msg "Wrayth shows the cleaned-up list the next time you log in. Don't change any Wrayth setting before then, " \
               "or Wrayth will save its own copy (with the blanks) over it; if that happens, run ;#{ME} cleanup again."
      end

    when 'reapply'
      colors = WhoHL::Colors.all
      _, n = store.() { |f| f.saved_professions.count { |p| f.set(p, f.names(p), colors[p]); true } }
      hl_msg "Put back #{n} profession list#{n == 1 ? '' : 's'} for #{whom}."
      hl_msg done_note if fe != :warlock

    when 'watch'
      raise WhoHL::Error, "watch is for Wrayth (Warlock keeps names on its own; Saga uses the import file)." unless fe == :wrayth
      path = check.()
      hl_msg "Watching Wrayth's settings file. When Wrayth saves over your scanned names, they're put back. ;kill #{ME} to stop."
      last = File.mtime(path)
      loop do
        sleep 2
        now = File.mtime(path) rescue next
        next if now == last
        sleep 0.5                              # let Wrayth finish writing
        file = reader.()
        unless file.missing.empty?
          colors = WhoHL::Colors.all
          store.() { |f| f.saved_professions.each { |p| f.set(p, f.names(p), colors[p]) } }
          hl_msg "Wrayth saved over #{file.missing.size} scanned name#{file.missing.size == 1 ? '' : 's'}; put them back."
        end
        last = File.mtime(path)
      end

    when 'add', 'drop'
      p = args.shift.to_s.downcase; prof_ok.(p)
      given = args.map(&:capitalize)
      bad = given.reject { |n| WhoHL.valid_name?(n) }
      raise WhoHL::Error, "Not valid names: #{bad.join(', ')}" unless bad.empty?
      raise WhoHL::Error, 'Give at least one name.' if given.empty?
      _, (total, skipped, conflicts) = store.() do |f|
        names = cmd == 'add' ? f.names(p) | given : f.names(p) - given
        sk = f.set(p, names, pick_color.(p))
        [f.names(p).size, sk, f.conflicts]
      end
      done = given - skipped - conflicts.keys
      verb = cmd == 'add' ? 'Added' : 'Dropped'
      hl_msg "#{done.empty? ? "Nothing #{verb.downcase}" : "#{verb} #{done.join(', ')}"}. #{p} now has #{total} name#{total == 1 ? '' : 's'} (#{whom})."
      hl_msg skipped_note.(skipped, list_name) unless skipped.empty?
      conflict_notes.(conflicts, p).each { |l| hl_msg l }
      hl_msg slot_note.(p) if cmd == 'add' && slot_note.(p)
      hl_msg done_note if fe != :warlock

    else
      profs = case cmd
              when 'all' then WhoHL::CATEGORIES
              when 'staff' then WhoHL::STAFF
              else [cmd]
              end
      profs.each { |p| prof_ok.(p) }
      check.()                                   # fail before sending WHO if the file or folder can't be used
      staff = nil                                # one plain WHO covers both staff lists
      profs.each do |p|
        if WhoHL::STAFF.include?(p)
          unless staff
            raw = hl_who('who')
            staff = WhoHL.staff_from(raw)
            unless raw.any? { |l| l =~ /Use WHO HELP|Active Players/i }
              hl_msg "WHO didn't answer as expected. Lines received:"
              raw.first(15).each { |l| hl_msg "  | #{l}" }
            end
          end
          found = staff[p] || []
          if found.empty?
            hl_msg "No #{WhoHL.label(p)}s on duty right now."
            next
          end
        else
          raw = hl_who("who profession #{p}")
          found = WhoHL.names_from(raw)
          if found.empty?
            hl_msg "No names found for #{p}. Lines received:"
            raw.first(15).each { |l| hl_msg "  | #{l}" }
            hl_msg 'If names were listed above, send these lines so the reader can be fixed.'
            next
          end
        end
        if opts[:dry]
          hl_msg "#{p}: #{found.size} found (dry run, nothing saved): #{found.join(', ')}"
          next
        end
        _, (before, after, skipped, conflicts) = store.() do |f|
          old = f.names(p)
          names = opts[:fresh] ? found : old | found
          sk = f.set(p, names, pick_color.(p))
          [old.size, f.names(p).size, sk, f.conflicts]
        end
        hl_msg "#{WhoHL.label(p)}: #{found.size} online, #{after} saved (#{after - before >= 0 ? '+' : ''}#{after - before}) for #{whom}."
        hl_msg skipped_note.(skipped, list_name) unless skipped.empty?
        conflict_notes.(conflicts, p).each { |l| hl_msg l }
        hl_msg slot_note.(p) if slot_note.(p)
        sleep 1 if profs.size > 1 && !WhoHL::STAFF.include?(p)
      end
      hl_msg done_note unless opts[:dry]
    end
  rescue WhoHL::Error => e
    hl_msg "ERROR: #{e.message}"
  end
end
